[Course "The Scandinavian Defense"]
[ChesslyCourse "d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab"]
[CourseColor "black"]
[CourseDescription "This is one of Black's best openings against 1.e4 below the master level. We almost always get a pretty easy game. Levy wins nearly 70% of the time with the Scandinavian Defense."]
[Chapter "The 3. Nc3 Classical Variation"]
[Study "White Plays 6.Bd2, 6. Be2, or 6.Bd3"]
[Video "/downloader/downloads/The Scandinavian Defense/01-01 The 3. Nc3 Classical Variation - White Plays 6.Bd2, 6. Be2, or 6.Bd3.mp4"]
[Orientation "black"]

{You have come to the right place if you want to have a solid and unexpected defense against 1.e4! The Scandinavian Defense is a polarizing opening: some are hesitant to play it, but it holds many secrets and ways to get a solid position. We determine the structure and style of play, and we can catch opponents off-guard who have been quick to dismiss it.}
1. e4 d5 2. exd5 {White captures. This is the most popular move you'll see. Our queen will recapture and get out early, which seems to break some opening principles, but it's okay, even desired, in the Scandinavian.} 2... Qxd5 3. Nc3 {This move is seen 77% of times, in around 57 million games. It is one of the most popular variations because it allows White to develop with a tempo. However, it may make White overconfident in their opening. In reality, we are in control. Our idea is to keep the queen active: 3...Qa5. [%csl Rd5]} 3... Qa5 4. d4 {d4 and Nf3 are White's most popular ideas, and this is the classical line. It follows traditional development with a pawn in the center. Our plan has gone into effect, and at this point, we'll develop our kingside knight.} 4... Nf6 5. Nf3 {White brings their knight out as well. This occurs 37% of the time, which equals about 745,000 games in the database. Before pushing ...e6, let's get our light-squared bishop out so it's not stuck later.} 5... Bf5 6. Bd2 {6. Bd2 is the most popular variation, being played 102,000 times in the database. White thinks they're threatening some discovered attacks. However, these aren't anything special.} (6. Bd3 {White could be tempted to propose a bishop trade. Their idea is that your bishop on f5 is too strong, so removing it is a good idea. 6. Bd3 has been played in 26% of games in the database, which is equal to 54,000 games. [%csl Rf5]} 6... Bxd3 7. Qxd3 {The queen recaptures. It's now important to control the d5-square while giving our dark-squared bishop some breathing room. [%csl Rd5]} 7... e6 8. O-O {White castles. Before ...Nbd7, 8...Be7 makes sense because our king needs to get to safety quickly.} 8... Be7 9. Bd2 {White may get their bishop on the same diagonal as your queen.} 9... O-O {But there's nothing scary about the bishop here. We're going to go ...Nbd7, c6 and bring the queen back if needed. We have a solid position and we're going to get a rook on the d-file. [%cal Gc7c6,Gb8d7,Ga5c7,Gf8d8]}) (6. Be2 {This won't be a problem at all. It's too passive because it doesn't challenge our bishop on f5 or aim to do something more aggressive.} 6... e6 7. O-O {White castles. To achieve the most optimal pawn structure, the move ...c6 is by far the best one here. We get to completely control the d5-square, and if we make it to the endgame, we may even be slightly better with some pressure.} 7... c6 8. Bf4 {White develops their bishop to f4.} 8... h6 {And we get ready to develop the rest of our pieces. White's Be2 allowed us to go for this classical structure. If we defend the middlegame properly, we should look forward to a solid endgame without weaknesses. [%cal Gf8e7,Ge8g8,Gb8d7,Gf6d5]}) 6... e6 7. Ne4 {For example, if 7. Ne4, there is a discovered attack. We have to save the queen. [%csl Ra5]} (7. Be2 {Setups with Be2 are quite natural, but they're passive. The bishop's most aggressive square is c4.} 7... Be7 {Since Be2 isn't a concern, all you have to do now is complete your development. 0-0, Nbd7, and c6 to bring the queen back if you need to. These are all standard plans in the Scandinavian. Your long-term goal is to get a rook on the d-file and control the d5-square thanks to the c6- and e6-pawns. [%cal Ge8g8,Gc7c6,Gb8d7,Ga5c7]}) (7. Bc4 {This is the most aggressive square for the bishop.} 7... Bb4 {This transposes into the 6. Bc4 line in the second study of this chapter.}) (7. Ne5 {You may also face this move. The e5-square is often used by White in the Scandinavian. You should target the weakness left after Ne5: the d4-pawn. But the b2-pawn is also unprotected, so that it will be targeted as well. [%csl Rd4,Rb2]} 7... Qb6 8. Qf3 {This is the best move for White. It's a little hard to find, but the idea is to pressure the b7-pawn. We should just defend our b7-pawn. [%csl Rb7]} 8... c6 9. O-O-O {White can castle. We can develop our knight, challenging the e5-knight. We cannot take on d4. If 9...Qxd4, 10. Bf4 and our queen is under attack, the rook has an open d-file and g4 would be on the board next.} 9... Nbd7 10. Nc4 {This was White's idea with Ne5 all along: to go to c4 and attack the queen. But we have the d8-square. [%csl Rb6]} 10... Qd8 11. h3 {White prevents the ...Bg4 skewer.} 11... Be7 {And we prepare our standard 0-0 plan. ...Nb6 is a good idea: we propose a trade which would be great for us after Nxb6 and ...axb6, with an open file towards the king. Of course, White won't do that if they're skilled, but ...Nb6 is useful to prepare ...Nfd5. If one knight is taken on d5, the other one takes its place. [%cal Ge8g8,Gd7b6,Gf6d5]}) 7... Qb6 8. Nxf6+ {White may trade knights here, thinking they're maybe getting some positional advantage by damaging our structure. No such advantage exists: our doubled f-pawns control many squares. They're a solid pawn island. In addition, we get the g-file.} 8... gxf6 9. Bc3 {9. Bc3, going on the same diagonal as our rook, isn't a big deal. [%csl Rh8]} 9... Nc6 {White's center will come under pressure. 10. d5 to attack our f-pawn would be a serious mistake because of 10...0-0-0 and the d-pawn would fall. After we castle, we can go for the ...Bb4 trade, since White's bishop is the only piece with some potential threats. [%cal Ge8c8,Gf8b4] [%csl Rd4]} *

[Chapter "The 3. Nc3 Classical Variation"]
[Study "White Plays 6.Bc4"]
[Video "/downloader/downloads/The Scandinavian Defense/01-02 The 3. Nc3 Classical Variation - White Plays 6.Bc4.mp4"]
[Orientation "black"]

{For Study 2, we'll continue our exploration of the Classical Line in the Scandinavian Defense. In particular, after White develops both knights, we'll explore an aggressive sixth move: the bishop coming to c4. This fights for the d5-square and aims at e6 and f7. Let's see how to defend. White begins with 1. e4, and we'll challenge them immediately with 1...d5.}
1. e4 d5 2. exd5 {White captures. This is the most popular move you'll see. We recapture.} 2... Qxd5 3. Nc3 {White gains a tempo and develops. With 3...Qa5 we prepare a pin after White plays d4. Our queen is safe, according to theory. [%csl Rd5]} 3... Qa5 4. d4 {d4, Nf3 and Nc3 are the Classical line. Our knight comes out to f6 to control d5. [%cal Gg1f3] [%csl Rc3]} 4... Nf6 5. Nf3 {White brings their knight out as well, following the principle of knights before bishops. We'll develop our light-squared bishop. f5 is a great square for it since it'll be outside of our pawn chain. We're also controlling the important e4-square. [%csl Re4]} 5... Bf5 6. Bc4 {This confrontational move is one of White's best. We should cover our weakness right away. White may have ideas of attacking and sacrificing on our f7-e6 diagonal later on, so we must be careful. [%csl Rf7]} 6... e6 7. O-O {The most popular move by far is castling. However, it shouldn't be a problem. We have time to create a blockade on the light squares and castle. We develop our knight. [%cal Gc7c6,Gb8d7]} (7. Bd2 {7. Bd2 is also popular to some degree. To counter the combination of Bc4 and Bd2, we'll play 7...Bb4.} 7... Bb4 8. a3 {White's most popular and natural move is to try and gain the bishop pair by forcing a trade. We'll take. [%csl Rb4]} (8. O-O {Castling is a natural move. We'll develop our queen's knight and likely transpose to another line.} 8... Nc6 9. a3 {a3 lands us directly in the 8. a3 line. We'll take on c3. [%csl Rb4]} 9... Bxc3 10. Bxc3 {White recaptures.} 10... Qb6) (8. Qe2 {White could continue by putting pressure on the e-file and castling queenside next. We'll castle quickly, getting our king off the e-file. [%csl Re6]} 8... O-O 9. O-O-O {White's setup is aggressive. We should expect a sharp game where we attack each other's kings. We can develop our knight with a plan: ...Bg4 to attack the d4-pawn.} 9... Nc6 10. a3 {This move doesn't help White. We now have a sacrifice for the attack. [%csl Rb4]} 10... Bxa3 11. bxa3 {White recaptures. We take with check.} 11... Qxa3+ 12. Kb1 {The king goes to the b-file. And now the point is revealed! The king is stuck on the b-file, so 12...b5 is really strong. We have Bxc2, Nb4, and Rb8 in the air.} 12... b5 13. Nxb5 {The knight captures and attacks the queen. [%csl Ra3]} 13... Rfb8 {But we don't have to move the queen away. We can pin the knight and our attack is devastating for White. [%cal Ra7a6] [%csl Rb5,Rb1]}) 8... Bxc3 9. Bxc3 {White recaptures and attacks our queen. And we'll make use of the b6-square where the queen is safe. Even though White has achieved the bishop pair, we'll start applying pressure to the white position. [%csl Ra5]} 9... Qb6 10. O-O {White castles. Let's bring our queen's knight out and put some pressure on d4.} (10. d5 {This move seems logical: White is trying to open things up since they played aggressively with Bc4. I like taking in the center. We'll be able to shut down White's initiative if we don't panic. Their king is in the center, too.} 10... exd5 11. Bxd5 {White recaptures, with some pressure on b7. Our queen cannot move away. However, we'll just castle. King safety is more important with those bishops roaming about. [%csl Rb7]} 11... O-O 12. Bxf6 {White may damage our pawns here. However, it is of no significant concern. We'll recapture, but despite the doubled pawns, we're solid. All our pieces (except our bishop) are defended.} 12... gxf6 {We'll recapture, but despite the doubled pawns, we're solid. All our pieces (except our bishop) are defended.} 13. O-O {White castles.} 13... Nd7 {Despite our damaged structure, White has lost the bishop pair and our pieces will have good squares. Our knight will be well located on e5, where the f6-pawn provides support. Rad8 will come with d-file pressure, and our queen is attacking b2. The bishop can also go to g6 to defend itself and the kingside. [%cal Gf5g6,Ga8d8,Gc7c6,Gd7e5] [%csl Rb2]}) (10. Qe2 {White may get the queen out of the d-file quickly to avoid counterplay. We'll castle to get the king off the e-file.} 10... O-O 11. O-O-O {We have opposite side castling. But we control the light squares in the center. We'll develop our other knight.} 11... Nc6 12. Ne5 {White centralizes their knight. Since they aren't threatening anything yet, we'll increase pressure on d4. Rad8 is the obvious move.} 12... Rad8 13. g4 {This pawn push looks natural, but it's a mistake. We can capture on e5 and win material. If gxf5, obviously ...Nxc4.} 13... Nxe5 14. dxe5 {White takes our knight back.} 14... Nxg4 {We recapture with the knight and are attacking f2. We're up a pawn and should be able to defend this. It's quite hard for White to deal with the pressure on f2 and h2. They'll probably have to play something like Rdf1. If they do, something like ...Qc6 is strong. 0-1 Meunier Pion, J (2175)-Rozman, L (2333) Chess.com INT 2022 [%cal Gd1f1,Gb6c6] [%csl Rf2,Rc4]}) 10... Nc6 11. Qe2 {You may see Qe2 here. White wants to develop their queen and connect rooks. This move also defends the bishop and puts some pressure on the e6-pawn. Because of the e-file pressure, we should just castle. [%cal Gf3h4] [%csl Gc4,Rf5]} (11. b4 {The idea behind b4 is to attack our knight. We shouldn't fear this attack, though. We castle into safety. [%cal Gb4b5] [%csl Rc6]} 11... O-O 12. b5 {White attacks our knight. We can go for a counterattack with Ne4. [%csl Rc6]} 12... Ne4 13. Bb2 {The bishop retreats to b2. We move our knight away to attack the other bishop.} 13... Na5 14. Be2 {The bishop retreats as well. It seems White's b4 and b5 operation wasn't so fruitful.} 14... Rfd8 {We can start moving our pieces into action. ...c5 is good to undermine the center and open our rook up. White will have to find a way to deal with the increasing pressure. [%cal Gc7c5] [%csl Rd4]}) (11. a4 {The idea behind this move is to bully our queen around. Whenever you play the Scandinavian, you should expect your opponent to try and attack your queen in some way. That's why you have to have ways to escape prepared. Here, 11...a5 stops White's advance. [%cal Ga4a5]} 11... a5 12. Bb5 {The bishop pins our knight. But we can castle. [%csl Rc6]} 12... O-O 13. Nd2 {White wants to, once again, attack our queen. We have time to attack c2 because our queen has an escape square on a7. [%cal Gd2c4]} 13... Nb4 14. Nc4 {White attacks us. We'll retreat to the bolthole on a7.} 14... Qa7 15. Ne3 {And White attacks our attacker and in turn defends c2. [%csl Gc2,Rf5]} 15... Bg6 {I really like how solid our position is and how vulnerable White's d4-pawn seems. We're blockading the square in front of it, and we can even go ...c6 and ...c5 to attack it. [%cal Gc7c6,Gc6c5,Gf8d8]}) (11. d5 {White pushes in the center and tries opening up the board. This also opens up an attack on our knight. This may seem dangerous, but we can pin the pawn! d5 can often be refuted in the Scandinavian if we have 0-0-0 available.} 11... O-O-O 12. Bxf6 {White takes on f6, trying to reduce the pressure on d5.} 12... gxf6 {But we simply recapture and we have an open g-file. We're also getting another attacker on the pawn with Be4. d5 and b2 are under attack. There's no way White will defend both. We should emerge up a pawn with a better position. [%cal Gh8g8,Rf5e4,Re6d5,Rb6b2] [%csl Gb2]}) (11. Re1 {White centralizes their rook. However, we can add pressure on d4 before castling.} 11... Rd8 {We'll castle next and bring our other rook to e8. We have the plan of ...Nd5 to attack the bishop on c3. Black has a stable position, which itself puts a pressure on White because they're not playing for a big advantage but are heading into a position where all three results are possible. [%cal Ge8g8,Gf6d5]}) 11... O-O 12. Rad1 {Centralizing rooks is normal in these positions, specially if there won't be any flank attacks. We'll do the same to increase our control of the d5-square. [%csl Gd5]} 12... Rad8 13. h3 {White prevents ...Bg4. [%csl Rg4]} 13... Ne4 {Black has an equal position out of the opening and we'll most likely get to take White's dark-squared bishop. Our plan will be to double up rooks on the d-file. If Nxc3, our queen will also be able to attack weaknesses with ...Qa5. [%cal Gb6a5]}) 7... Nbd7 8. Bd2 {White places their bishop opposite our queen. With ...c6, we clear a path for our queen to retreat.} 8... c6 9. Re1 {White may start adding e-file pressure. We get the queen out of any potential discovered attacks. [%csl Re8]} (9. Nd5 {White has this discovered attack. But since we played ...c6, we should be fine. We can simply retreat the queen to d8.} 9... Qd8 10. Nxf6+ {White has to trade.} 10... Nxf6 {We recapture and we'll develop all our pieces in a coherent manner: centralizing our rooks and fighting for control of d5. [%cal Gf8e7,Ge7d6,Ge8g8] [%csl Gd5]}) 9... Qc7 10. h3 {White aims to restrict our bishop on f5 and target it with Nh4. [%cal Gf3h4] [%csl Rf5]} 10... h6 {We'll go ...Be7 or ...Bd6 and castle kingside. With kings castled on the same side, there will be no tactical race for checkmate. Rather, we're looking to develop all our pieces, control d5, maybe expand on the queenside and centralize our rooks. [%cal Gf5h7,Gf8e7,Ga7a5,Ge8g8]} *

[Chapter "The Critical 6.Ne5"]
[Study "All Lines"]
[Video "/downloader/downloads/The Scandinavian Defense/02-01 The Critical 6.Ne5 - All Lines.mp4"]
[Orientation "black"]

{Welcome to Chapter 2 of our course in the Scandinavian. In this chapter, we'll analyze White's critical Ne5 jump on move 6, which has become one of the most consistent weapons against the Qa5 Scandinavian. Let's dive into it.}
1. e4 d5 2. exd5 {White takes. We'll take back with the queen, of course.} 2... Qxd5 3. Nc3 {White will most likely develop with tempo on the queen. And, as in the last chapter, we are opting for the Qa5 Scandinavian. [%csl Rd5]} 3... Qa5 4. d4 {The most common is for White to place a pawn in the center, and now their knight is pinned. Let's begin developing pieces with Nf6. [%csl Rc3,Re1]} 4... Nf6 5. Nf3 {White activates both knights. Our light-squared bishop has a nice place on f5.} 5... Bf5 6. Ne5 {6.Ne5 is only played 3% of the time in the Lichess database under 2000 ELO, but is played 27% in the Masters database! This is the most testing move from White that we could encounter. We do have the ...c6 resource here, so we can retreat our queen if needed.} 6... c6 7. Bc4 {7.Bc4 is the second most popular move, played 25% of the time, but definitely the most sensible one. White is simply attacking the f7-pawn with two pieces. Of course, the most straightforward defense is ...e6, and the best one. [%csl Rf7]} (7. Nc4 {White retreats the knight 17% of the time. This would have been a problem if hadn't we played ...c6 on the last move. Now, we can just retreat back to d8.} 7... Qd8 8. Be2 {White develops and prepares for castling. Also, because the bishop is on e2, g4 is now in the cards. We should be careful, and prepare a retreating square for our bishop.} 8... h6 9. O-O {White castles...} 9... e6 {And ...e6 is natural to continue development. We'll develop the knight to d7 next, the bishop to either e7 or d6 and castle. A comfortable position for Black. [%cal Gf8e7,Ge8h8]}) 7... e6 8. g4 {For an opponent that's not prepared, g4 is definitely a big surprise! White attacks our bishop and forces us to retreat. This enables them to start a quick kingside pawn storm with tempo. [%csl Rf5]} (8. O-O {If White castles, they are basically telling us they want a calm and equal game. Once again, the knight on e5 should be challenged.} 8... Nbd7 9. Qe2 {If they keep the tension, that's fine too. We don't need to capture and let them recapture with tempo.} 9... Rd8 {We can simply get the rook into the game. In some cases, we could even play ...Nb6 to attack both the bishop and the pawn on d4. Development and castling next, either way. [%cal Gd7b6] [%csl Gf8,Ge8,Rc4,Rd4]}) (8. Bd2 {White can go here, of course, but it's not scary and the main idea is supposed to be g2-g4. You simply play...} 8... Nbd7 {And if the Nc3 moves, just retreat your queen to c7 or d8. [%csl Rc3]}) 8... Bg6 9. h4 {The most logical move for White is to stay consistent in hunting our bishop. We don't have to stop the threat of h5. Instead, we can challenge the knight on e5. [%csl Rg6]} (9. Bd2 {The 9.Bd2 line will follow the same moves we've seen in the previous line, but we'll show the variation in more depth. First, we'll offer the knight trade as per usual.} 9... Nbd7 10. Qe2 {It makes more sense for White to defend e5 now, because it enables them to castle long on the next move. Now we can activate the bishop to b4.} 10... Bb4 11. O-O-O {White castles long. This is how you could think: Now we've activated the bishop to pressure the knight, it would be great if we could add even more pressure with ...Nd5. But if we play it right now, White can capture on d7. Therefore, we play Nxe5.} 11... Nxe5 12. dxe5 {The pawn recaptures, and now we have an easy decision on where to jump with the knight. [%csl Rf6]} 12... Nd5 13. Ne4 {White evades the pressure and adds a defender to the d2-bishop. Trades are about to happen.} 13... Bxd2+ 14. Rxd2 {Rook takes. Now, notice that since we've traded the dark-squared bishops, White is threatening to infiltrate with Nd6+. Therefore, we'll exchange minor pieces on e4.} 14... Bxe4 15. Qxe4 {Queen takes.} 15... O-O-O {After all is said and done, we'll castle long with a strong knight in the center. A good idea would be to push on the kingside. Something like ...g5 and ...Nf4 would give the knight a great outpost. The knight could also retreat to b6 to hit the bishop on c4. [%cal Gg7g5,Gh7h5,Gd5f4,Gd5b6]}) (9. Nxg6 {White capturing now doesn't make too much sense.} 9... hxg6 {They just give us the open h-file for the rook. And we'll gladly take it, and continue by developing the knight and castling long! Now White will have to permanently worry about the h-file. [%cal Gb8d7,Ge8c8] [%csl Gh8,Rh2]}) 9... Nbd7 10. Nxd7 {But what if White trades and then plays h5? We recapture with the knight.} (10. Qe2 {If White avoids the trade and defends the knight, that's actually a mistake! We'll take on e5.} 10... Nxe5 11. dxe5 {White takes, and now we can put pressure on a pinned piece. Ne4, moving our attacked knight, is the way forward. [%csl Rf6,Rc3]} 11... Ne4 12. Rh3 {If Rh3 is played, controlling the third rank, here's a move to remember.} 12... h5 {After ...h5, Black is winning. If they capture, our bishop and our rook will get active. If g5, Bf5 will prove hugely disruptive. Otherwise, we can capture on g4 and if the queen recaptures, there's a skewer on f5! [%cal Gh5g4,Ge2g4,Gg6f5] [%csl Rg4]}) 10... Nxd7 11. h5 {Luckily, h5 only grabs space but doesn't trap the bishop. The e4-square is available, since the c3-knight is pinned. [%csl Rc3,Re1,Rg6]} 11... Be4 12. O-O {Castling is an interesting move recommended by the computer. The threat of capturing our bishop is reintroduced since the knight is no longer pinned. We should retreat the bishop to d5. [%csl Re4]} (12. Rh3 {12.Rh3 gives up any ever so slight advantage that White might have had. We've got a nice improving move. Nb6 brings the knight into the game, attacks the c4-bishop, and increases our central control.} 12... Nb6 13. Bb3 {Once the bishop retreats, let's get our bishop out of the way and offer a trade.} 13... Bd5 14. Bd2 {They can activate the c1-bishop instead. We have already seen the ...Bb4 concept in response.} 14... Bb4 15. a3 {In this case, our whole idea if White attacks our bishop is to trade. [%csl Rb4]} 15... Bxc3 16. Bxc3 {White can recapture with the bishop and attack our queen.} 16... Qa6 {But we can slide it to a6, where it's completely safe. Next, we'll castle long with an equal position and good light-square control. [%cal Ge8c8]}) 12... Bd5 13. Nxd5 {If White takes, we'll recapture with the c-pawn.} 13... cxd5 14. Bd3 {And White retreats to d3, having traded the knight for our bishop. This is the critical position of this modern variation, as has been tested in many GM games. We have a very sound Caro-esque structure, but White has the bishop pair and space on the kingside. Let's develop the bishop.} 14... Bd6 15. c3 {A harmonious move for White is c3, placing pawns on the opposite color of their extra bishop. We should get our king out of the center by castling queenside, so we can challenge White's advanced kingside pawns later.} (15. Qf3 {A move worth analyzing is 15.Qf3, played by Alireza Firouzja against Magnus Carlsen. The queen is eyeing the kingside. Black has an interesting move here, which aims to solve the problem of our kingside. [%csl Rf7]} 15... O-O-O 16. Be3 {White could choose not to go into the 16.Qxf7 line and defend the center while developing. Well, we've got another trick for them! First of all, we get our king off the c-file.} (16. Qxf7 {As mentioned, White can take the pawn for free. The queen is also attacking the e6-pawn now. Well, we want to attack the central d4-pawn and actively defend e6!} 16... Qb6 17. Kg2 {Kg2 kills the Bh2+ discovered attack tactic, so now White is threatening to capture on e6 again. Now it's time to defend it for real, with Rde8.} 17... Rde8 18. c3 {White finally defends the d-pawn, and we'll attack the queen, giving up yet another pawn!} 18... Rhf8 19. Qxg7 {The only safe square for White is to capture on g7.} 19... Rg8 {A new line of attack has opened! We attack the queen again, this time on the g-file, and skewer the g4-pawn. White is going to lose that pawn, but the position will remain equal. Confusing and full of poison, but equal. [%csl Rg7,Rg4]}) 16... Kb8 17. Qxf7 {If White does take in this line, however, that's a losing blunder! White is losing by force after we attack the queen with the h-rook.} 17... Rhf8 18. Qxg7 {If the queen captures on g7, it's only opened up a new line of attack for us.} (18. Qxe6 {If White captures on e6...} 18... Rf6 {The queen is completely trapped. Oops! [%csl Gf6,Re6]}) 18... Rg8 {We can slide to g8 and attack the queen again. Once it moves, the g4-pawn will be hanging with check. This is winning for Black. [%csl Gg8,Gd8,Rg7,Rg4,Rg1]}) (15. a4 {White anticipates us castling long and starts a pre-emptive attack. We won't be playing according to their plan and instead chip away at the kingside structure. [%csl Rh5,Rg4]} 15... g6 16. Bd2 {Once White hits our queen, think about where we want our play to be focused around based on our last move, since we want to achieve...Qh4. Therefore, we'll play Qd8.} 16... Qd8 17. Kg2 {Of course, it's not that easy. Kg2 prepares Rh1 if we infiltrate on h4.} 17... Qf6 {We can settle with the f6-square, though. Note that we aren't really attacking the d4-pawn for now because of Bc3 ideas. We are simply improving our position. Next, we'll castle long. [%cal Ge8c8]}) (15. Bd2 {Another logical idea by White is to develop with tempo on our queen. We'll retreat to b6 and hit some pawns.} 15... Qb6 16. c3 {16.c3 sufficiently connects the pawns and defends the center. Capturing on b2 is too dangerous because of Rb1 ideas. But we have another concrete plan.} 16... Bc7 {We'll retreat to c7, with the idea of placing the queen on d6 and threatening checkmate. Apart from that, kingside ideas to undermine White's structure could be ...g6 and ...f5, as well as ...Nf6. In that case, we'll probably castle long. Frankly, you can castle either way in this line in general, but there's a long game awaiting. [%cal Ge8c8,Ge8g8,Gg7g6,Gf7f5,Gd7f6,Gb6d6]}) 15... O-O-O 16. Qf3 {If White goes for Qf3 now, we'll give up the f7-pawn.} (16. b4 {Playing with tempo, White could try to attack us on the queenside once we castle. No worries, we just retreat to the safest square, c7. [%csl Ra5]} 16... Qc7 17. Be3 {White develops the bishop, perhaps aiming for Rc1.} 17... Kb8 {But nothing to worry about after the prophylactic ...Kb8. Again, important to remember that we've got counterplay in the form of ...g6 and ...f5, pushing the kingside pawns! [%cal Gg7g6,Gf7f5] [%csl Gb8]}) 16... Rde8 {But we'll prepare for it with ...Rde8, so that the e6-pawn is protected. White can't capture on f7 because of the same line as in the 15.Qf3 branch. Next, we'd like to put ...g6 and ...f5 into motion. Another already mentioned common idea is transferring the queen to the kingside via d8 and h4. [%cal Ga5d8,Gd8h4,Gg7g6,Gf7f5] [%csl Ge6,Gg7,Gf7]} *

[Chapter "Other White 5th Moves"]
[Study "White Goes 5.Bd2 And Others"]
[Video "/downloader/downloads/The Scandinavian Defense/03-01 Other White 5th Moves - White Goes 5.Bd2 And Others.mp4"]
[Orientation "black"]

{Welcome to the third chapter. Let's look at a very popular variation: 5. Bd2, placing the bishop opposite the queen right away. Among the 5th move options we'll explore, we'll also look at other less popular ones. Let's get right into it, with 1.e4 d5!}
1. e4 d5 2. exd5 {White captures, as usual. We'll take back with the queen.} 2... Qxd5 3. Nc3 {White usually gains a tempo with Nc3. We slide our queen to a5. [%csl Rd5]} 3... Qa5 4. d4 {White grabs some central squares. Let's start getting our pieces out with Nf6.} 4... Nf6 5. Bd2 {This happens approximately 43% of the time in more than 1.5 million games. White wants to set up a discovered attack on our queen. We can ignore it for now, and develop with tempo instead.} (5. Bd3 {White develops the light-squared bishop. Some players may choose to delay Bd2 by playing another developing move beforehand. Alternatively, White may simply be focused on castling kingside quickly. We'll play the natural developing move Nc6.} 5... Nc6 6. Ne2 {White protects the d-pawn with Nge2. White plans to meet ...Bg4 with f3, but that's exactly what we're going for. [%csl Rd4]} (6. Nf3 {White could protect the d-pawn with Nf3. Our bishop comes into the action now, pinning the knight. [%csl Rd4]} 6... Bg4 7. Be3 {White adds a further protector to the d4-pawn. Let's challenge White's center with the pawn push e5. [%csl Rd4]} 7... e5 8. dxe5 {White's d4-pawn was hanging, so they traded. We recapture with the knight.} 8... Nxe5 9. Be2 {White retreats and defends. Let's grab the open d-file, hitting the white queen!} 9... Rd8 10. Bd2 {White retreats and breaks the pin on the c3-knight. The g4-bishop doesn't have a better use, so let's trade off on f3 and remove the challenge to our strong central knight.} 10... Bxf3 11. Bxf3 {Recapturing.} 11... Bb4 {Black is doing very well here. We are ready to castle, bring the king's rook to the e-file, and try to use our piece activity. [%cal Ge8h8,Gd8d1] [%csl Ge5,Rc3]}) 6... Bg4 7. f3 {White attacks our bishop, breaking the pin on the e2-knight. [%csl Rg4]} 7... Bf5 {We'll retreat to f5 and offer a trade. Black aims to castle queenside and add pressure to the d4-pawn. [%cal Ga5f5,Ge8c8] [%csl Rd4]}) (5. Bf4 {White goes for a move which doesn't happen often, but is still a natural developing move. We'll occupy the usual square and attack the queen with our bishop.} 5... Bg4 6. Be2 {White offers a trade of bishops. We'll accept. Retreating wouldn't give us any benefits.} 6... Bxe2 7. Nxe2 {White recaptures.} 7... Nc6 {And as usual, we'll bring the knight to c6, castle queenside and put pressure on the d4-pawn. [%cal Ge7e6,Ge8c8] [%csl Rd4]}) 5... Bg4 6. f3 {White blocks the diagonal and attacks our bishop, which occurs in 28% of the games. We'll just retreat to a safe square, where our bishop won't be targeted. Note that after we retreat, White no longer has f3 for their king's knight. [%csl Rg4]} (6. Be2 {White offers to trade the light-squared bishops, which is the most common move here (36%). We'll accept.} 6... Bxe2 7. Qxe2 {White recaptures with the queen, so that Nf3 will be possible. Obviously both knight captures are also fine, but we can simply finish our development and get your king to safety. Also don't blunder the queen after Ncxe2! Here we'll play Nc6 and attack the d-pawn.} 7... Nc6 8. Nf3 {White defends it. Now we play the standard move e6 to control the center and open up the bishop. [%csl Rd4]} 8... e6 9. O-O {White castles and connects the rooks.} 9... Qf5 {We bring our queen to a more active square. We are ready to castle queenside and put more pressure on the d4-pawn. [%cal Gf5c2,Ge8c8] [%csl Rd4]}) (6. Nf3 {White blocks the attack by developing the knight, which happens 28% of the time. We'll put further pressure on the knight by bringing our queen to f5. [%csl Rf3]} 6... Qf5 7. Bd3 {White attacks our queen, which is the second most popular move (26%). We can go ahead and remove the knight on f3, counter-attacking White's queen. [%csl Rf3,Rf5]} (7. Be2 {This is the most common move here with a 79% occurrence — supporting the f3-knight.} 7... Nc6 {We'll simply develop and go for ... O-O-O with ...e5 to come. [%cal Ge7e5,Ge8c8] [%csl Rd4]}) 7... Bxf3 8. Bxf5 {White captures our queen. Naturally, we will capture theirs in return.} (8. gxf3 {White grabs the bishop instead.} 8... Qd7 {But ...Qd7 with ...e6 and ...Nc6 will put pressure on the d4-pawn. White's kingside pawns are badly fractured. [%cal Ge7e6,Gb8c6] [%csl Rd4,Rf3,Rf2]}) 8... Bxd1 9. Rxd1 {White captures the bishop. Now the common idea of ...e6 comes with tempo on the f5-bishop!} 9... e6 10. Bd3 {The bishop retreats.} 10... Nc6 {And c6 is once again a good square for the knight. Here it is eyeing both the d4-pawn and the b4-square. [%cal Gc6b4,Ge8c8] [%csl Rd4]}) 6... Bd7 7. Bc4 {White develops the light-squared bishop to c4. Now it's time to get our queen out of the aim of the bishop on d2, while also pressuring an undefended pawn.} 7... Qb6 8. Nge2 {White is developing. Let's open up the dark-squared bishop and control some central squares.} 8... e6 9. Be3 {White once again lines up the bishop with our queen. No worries, the bishop isn't defended, so there isn't any discovery.} 9... Be7 10. Qd3 {White defends the bishop and prepares to castle queenside. Let's focus on development.} (10. Qd2 {White defends the bishop with Qd2 and aims to play d5 in the future. Let's fearlessly grab the b2-pawn!} 10... Qxb2 11. Rb1 {White attacks our queen. Our next move is forced.} 11... Qa3 12. Rxb7 {White regains the b-pawn. We can simply castle and get our king into safety.} 12... O-O 13. O-O {White castles also. Grabbing on c7 wouldn't end well after Ne8 14.Rb7 Nd6 and the rook finds itself roaming in the wilderness.} 13... Nc6 {White's bishops aren't doing much, and the d5-square is under our control. [%csl Gd5,Re3,Rc4]}) 10... Nc6 11. O-O-O {White gets their king out of the center. We have a great square for our knight now.} (11. d5 {We shouldn't be worried about d5 here. It looks dangerous, but we can counter-attack the white queen. [%csl Rc6,Rb6]} 11... Nb4 12. Qd2 {White doesn't want to simplify by taking our queen.} 12... Qxe3 {A cold shower for White! ...Nxc2 will win the queen back and we'll emerge a pawn up! [%cal Gb4c2,Rd2e3] [%csl Ge3,Re1]}) 11... Nb4 12. Qd2 {The queen retreats. And now we get out of the e3-bishop's line of fire and attack the c4-bishop.} 12... Qc6 13. Bb3 {White retreats.} 13... a5 {And we expand on the queenside with ...a5. We are ready to castle short, expand further with a4 and we have good control over the d5-square. [%cal Ge8g8,Ga5a4,Gb4d5] [%csl Gd5]} *

[Chapter "White Doesn't Play Nf3"]
[Study "White Plays 6.f3 or 6.Nf3"]
[Video "/downloader/downloads/The Scandinavian Defense/04-01 White Doesn't Play Nf3 - White Plays 6.f3 or 6.Nf3.mp4"]
[Orientation "black"]

{It's time to analyze positions after 5. Bc4 by White in the Qa5 Scandinavian. We'll attack the queen right away with 5... Bg4. In this study, we'll see what to do if White plays f3 or Nf3 on their sixth move. These two are the most popular ways to block our attack on the queen, and I've prepared creative ways to counter both of them. Let's get started with the opening moves 1.e4 d5.}
1. e4 d5 2. exd5 {White takes. We'll recapture with the queen.} 2... Qxd5 3. Nc3 {White activates the knight with tempo... We'll slide the queen to a5. [%csl Rd5]} 3... Qa5 4. d4 {Expanding in the center is natural. We'll bring out our king's knight.} 4... Nf6 5. Bc4 {A popular move instead of 5. Nf3 is Bc4, developing the bishop to the most active square. This has been played around 8% of times in more than 300,000 games. If we play in the most natural way, White will develop the knight to f3 and then the bishop to d2. However, here's where we spice things up. We bring our bishop out, attacking the white queen! [%csl Rf7]} 5... Bg4 6. f3 {f3 is the most common move, just like in Chapter 3. It has been played around more than 50% of time. Our bishop will be solid on d7. [%csl Rg4]} (6. Nf3 {Around 25% of games go like this. White is blocking the attack but also developing. Let's continue to develop.} 6... Nc6 7. O-O {You'll most likely see White castle here. This is already bad for them because in the long term White will not be able to defend the d4-pawn. We'll castle and increase the pressure on the pawn. [%csl Rd4]} (7. h3 {White may attempt to get rid of the pin quickly. This is one of the best approaches, but we have a strong response, since White hasn't castled yet. Qh5 pins the h-pawn to the king's rook. [%csl Rf3,Rg4]} 7... Qh5 8. Be2 {White may try to break the pin on the knight. We can now get our king into safety and pressure the d4-pawn. [%csl Rf3,Rd4]} (8. Be3 {8. Be3 is a popular attempt to overprotect the d4-pawn. However, we can nevertheless castle: ...e5 is coming. White may all of a sudden start feeling discomfort in this position. A pin on the knight, on the h-file and on the d-file!} 8... O-O-O 9. Qe2 {Many people may resort to Qe2, taking the queen off the d-file. But a natural-looking move like Qe2 loses. We'll play e5. They might have escaped the d-file, but they didn't escape the pin on the knight.} 9... e5 10. O-O-O {If White castles, thinking they revive a threat on our bishop on g4, they will lose material.} 10... Bxf3 {We first remove a defender of d4. We're ready to play a fork on d4 and go up material. We are completely winning! [%cal Re5d4] [%csl Gd4]}) 8... O-O-O 9. Be3 {White could try to overprotect d4. This strategy often fails. Our rook is nicely placed on the d-file, so we are ready to strike in the center with e5. [%csl Gd8,Rd1,Rd4]} (9. O-O {White may castle here. This is a logical move because it removes the h-file pin and revives the threat on the bishop. But we don't have to respond because we have the clever move of taking on d4.} 9... Nxd4 10. Nxd4 {White takes on d4. We'll go for the trade of bishops here. White's coordination is damaged.} 10... Bxe2 11. Ncxe2 {The knight captures back.} (11. Qxe2 {If the queen takes, the knight on d4 lacks defense.} 11... Rxd4 12. Qxh5 {White trades.} 12... Nxh5 {And we recapture. We have a slight edge thanks to the central pawn that we're up. [%cal Gb7b6,Gc1e3,Gd4c4,Ge7e6]}) 11... e5 {But we have a pin. If 12. Be3 we've got 12... Bc5. This is sound for us. White will likely regain their pawn, but we've successfully developed all our pieces. [%cal Gc1e3,Gf8c5]}) 9... e5 10. d5 {White has to push. But they are not even threatening to take our knight! Let's get our last piece out into the game and pin the c3-knight. [%csl Rd8,Rd1,Rc3]} 10... Bb4 11. O-O {White castles. This puts us on the spot because the g4-bishop is hanging and our queen may not be in the safest of squares. Also, the c3-knight is no longer pinned. We have the enterprising sacrifice Bxh3, and a chaotic position results.} 11... Bxh3 12. gxh3 {White doesn't have any useful discoveries, so they take the piece. Their king will be exposed in this middlegame, and we'll attack it. We'll now capture on c3, removing d5's defender.} 12... Bxc3 13. bxc3 {White recaptures. We don't want to blunder with Qxh3 because of ...Ng5 tactics. Instead, we'll activate our rook and capture on d5. [%cal Gh5h3,Rf3g5]} 13... Rxd5 14. Qe1 {The queen goes to e1. We can gain a tempo on the knight with e4. Our aim is to remove some defenders of the king. [%csl Rf3,Rg1]} 14... e4 15. Nh2 {White's best move is to keep the knight on the kingside. Taking on h3 is now safe and it's the best square for our queen. [%csl Rh3]} 15... Qxh3 16. Kh1 {This line is for illustration. 16. Kh1 is tricky to find. We'll play h5. The idea is to support the coming attack with Ng4.} 16... h5 17. Rg1 {The rook goes to g1. White wants to defend nearer the king and maybe propose a trade or play Bf1 to eject the queen. We'll attack the pinned knight with Ng4. [%cal Ge1f1,Ge2f1]} 17... Ng4 18. Bf4 {This is the best defense of the knight. Instead, Rg2 allows ...Nce5 with much more pressure. [%cal Gf4h2] [%csl Gh2]} 18... Nce5 {We're constantly posing challenges and bringing more pieces to the kingside. If White ever takes on g4, they'll open up our rook on h8. The position is equal because the engine finds incredible resources to defend, but your opponents are either lucky or extremely well-prepared if they manage to last this long!}) (7. Bd2 {White may decide to place the bishop on d2 at this point. But there are still many squares our queen can go to, so there are no issues.} 7... O-O-O {Hence, we should simply castle. Our rook is placed on the d-file, with pressure on d4. White may get into trouble quickly. Our queen is always safe - White can move the c3-knight to target it with a discovery, but our queen won't get trapped. [%csl Rd4]}) (7. Be3 {White may have the idea of overprotecting d4. However, this passive approach does not work. We have a very active resource here, and we push e5. [%csl Rd4]} 7... e5 8. dxe5 {8. dxe5 allows our c6-knight to get right into the action. Our rook also gets a clear d-file. We'll recapture, with pressure on the knight and bishop. [%csl Rf3,Rc4]} (8. d5 {White may be tempted to push the d-pawn. [%csl Rc6]} 8... O-O-O {But they simply do not have the firepower to defend the pawn. We have ideas of ...e4 and if Qc1 we can easily win d5. [%cal Gd1c1,Re5e4,Rf6d5] [%csl Rd5,Rd8,Rd1]}) 8... Nxe5 9. Be2 {If White breaks the pin and protects the knight more, we can continue improving our pieces with tempo.} 9... Rd8 {Our powerful rook enters the d-file. The queen has to move away and no squares are desirable. If White blocks with 10.Bd2 we can simply keep developing our pieces. [%cal Gd1c1,Gd1b1,Gg4f3] [%csl Rd1]}) 7... O-O-O 8. d5 {Black has a pleasant advantage.} 8... e6 {There's no way White can keep their pawn. You should go on to convert this. [%csl Rd5]}) 6... Bd7 7. Ne2 {This is played nearly 48% of the time. If White instead plays 7.Bd2, it transposes to the next study. I recommend challenging the center with ...c5.} 7... c5 8. d5 {The reason we want to see d5 on the board is that the pawn is overextended. Our pawns will now overrun White on the queenside. [%csl Rd5]} (8. O-O {It's possible that White may ignore the central tension. This is reasonable because the pawn has sufficient defenders, but we should take nevertheless. [%csl Rd4]} 8... cxd4 9. Nxd4 {White recaptures.} 9... e6 {And we block the bishop's view of f7, while opening a diagonal for our other bishop. We are about to complete development and we're the only side left with a central pawn, the e-pawn. We've equalized with the black pieces in the Scandinavian! [%cal Gf8e7,Ge8g8,Ga5c7]}) (8. Be3 {White could add another piece into the mix and further defend d4. But as long as we can remove White's central pawn, we're satisfied with our position. [%csl Rd4]} 8... cxd4 9. Qxd4 {The queen recaptures. White's pieces are all out and only their rooks need to be developed. We'll play e6 and prepare to activate our f8-bishop.} 9... e6 10. O-O-O {White castles, with some pressure on the d-file. [%csl Rd7]} 10... Be7 {That's why we'll prepare castling. We'll of course delay Nc6 until we can make sure it's safe. If we can get a queen trade after something like Nc6 we'll take it without hesitation. [%cal Ge8g8,Gb8c6]}) 8... b5 9. Bd3 {The bishop avoids its demise by going to d3. Our plan is to remove all of the defenders of the d-pawn to undermine it with greater effect. We start by attacking the bishop once more.} 9... c4 10. Be4 {The bishop goes to e4. Let's continue the queenside expansion with b4!} 10... b4 11. Nb1 {White's knight is forced to a bad square. Now it's time to get rid of White's best-placed piece. [%csl Re4]} 11... Nxe4 12. fxe4 {Even though White adds a defender to the d-pawn, this doesn't solve White's overextended pawn placement.} 12... e6 {The undermining process begins. We attack d5 and get ready to bring our dark-squared bishop into the game. [%cal Gf8c5] [%csl Rd5]} *

[Chapter "White Doesn't Play Nf3"]
[Study "White Plays 6.Ne2"]
[Video "/downloader/downloads/The Scandinavian Defense/04-02 White Doesn't Play Nf3 - White Plays 6.Ne2.mp4"]
[Orientation "black"]

{This study introduces our subject with White blocking the attack with 6. Ne2. Our bishop isn't forced back right away: we'll develop our queenside knight with 6... Nc6, but this alters things a bit. We've got a more dynamic position after that. We'll start with 1.e4 d5.}
1. e4 d5 2. exd5 {White takes. We'll recapture with the queen.} 2... Qxd5 3. Nc3 {White activates the knight with tempo... And we'll move the queen to a5. [%csl Rd5]} 3... Qa5 4. d4 {Expanding in the center comes naturally. Let's develop the g8-knight.} 4... Nf6 5. Bc4 {A popular move instead of 5. Nf3 is Bc4, developing the bishop to the most active square. This has been played around 8% of times in more than 300,000 games. If we play in the most natural way, White will develop the knight to f3 and the bishop to d2 next. However, here's where we spice things up with rapid development. Bg4 hits the white queen. [%csl Rf7]} 5... Bg4 6. Ne2 {This is a weird maneuver, but it's not bad. It has happened in about 16% of games. Yet again, the plan of ...Nc6 followed by queenside castling is strong.} 6... Nc6 7. f3 {This is quite disruptive for our coordination because we don't want to play Bd7 here. But we don't mind retreating to e6. The doubled e-pawns will prove to be an asset. [%csl Rg4]} (7. Be3 {7.Be3 is also played 6% of the time, and it's an inaccuracy for the same reason as the previous moves. It's really easy for White to make a mistake after 5...Nc6 by us. That's why I love it! [%csl Gd4]} 7... O-O-O {Of course, we'll castle long and immediately attack the d4-pawn. We can play e7-e5 next, even if White plays 8.f3, due to tactics in the center. [%cal Ge7e5]}) (7. Bd2 {7. Bd2 is played only 6% of the time but has a concrete idea behind it. White simply lines up the bishop with our queen. However, we should still long castle here.} 7... O-O-O 8. f3 {First, White attacks the bishop. We'll retreat to the natural f5-square. [%csl Rg4]} 8... Bf5 9. Nd5 {An interesting idea by White is 9.Nd5, and it's important to be prepared against it. There's a discovered attack on our queen. It may feel suffocated, but it will survive on a4! [%csl Ra5]} 9... Qa4 10. Bb3 {Apart from b3, White could attack our queen with Bb3. But that also opens up a new square to escape to. [%csl Ra4]} (10. b3 {White going to b3 doesn't manage to trap our queen, after all. They've just opened up the a3-square for us and given us the diagonal to retreat along. [%csl Ra4]} 10... Qa3 11. Nxf6 {Taking on f6 is the most logical.} 11... gxf6 {And we'll recapture with the g-pawn for a reason! We want to leave the option of ...e5 open, which is our main pawn break in most of the positions here. Our queen is fine and White's d4-pawn is vulnerable. [%cal Ge7e5,Gc6d4] [%csl Rd4]}) 10... Qa6 {Which is a6. What will White do about the knight on d5 now? [%csl Rd5]}) 7... Be6 8. Bxe6 {The most challenging and critical move is White capturing on e6. We'll recapture with the idea of ...e5 later.} 8... fxe6 9. O-O {White castling gives us the green light to move forward with our plans. We'll castle queenside, and now e5 is in the air.} (9. Bd2 {Even if White threatens some discoveries...} 9... e5 {We'll still push, and recapture with the queen before castling queenside. [%csl Rd4]}) (9. Nf4 {White could try go for our e6-pawn instantly, thinking it's a weakness they could easily win. But we're prepared for that, of course. Castling queenside will likely be a surprise to the white player! [%csl Re6]} 9... O-O-O 10. Nxe6 {If White captures...} 10... Rd6 {We've got a rook lift with tempo, attacking the knight. If White takes our bishop, we recapture and double our rooks, ready to smash the center. White is in trouble here. If we need, we'll also add Qf5. [%cal Ga5f5,Gd6e6] [%csl Rd4]}) 9... O-O-O 10. Be3 {10. Be3 does nothing to prevent our pawn advance, yet is the most common move in this position in the amateur database.} (10. f4 {White can stop us from playing e5, in which case we will play...} 10... g6 {This. We can fianchetto the dark-squared bishop, which will serve as another attacker of the d4-pawn. All in all, an unclear position. The good thing is that if f4 is occupied by a pawn, it cannot be occupied by a piece. White cannot play Nf4 to attack the e6-pawn. [%cal Gf8g7] [%csl Rd4]}) 10... e5 {And we'll go for it right away. We've achieved all our goals. We can capture on d4 and play e7-e5 immediately once again! [%csl Rd4]} *

[Chapter "Move 4 Sidelines After 3.Nc3"]
[Study "White Plays 4.Nf3 or 4.Bc4"]
[Video "/downloader/downloads/The Scandinavian Defense/05-01 Move 4 Sidelines After 3.Nc3 - White Plays 4.Nf3 or 4.Bc4.mp4"]
[Orientation "black"]

{In previous chapters, we've seen the most natural setups White can go for: the move d4 is played at some point. However, it's possible that White chooses to develop a minor piece at move 4 and then not play d4. We'll explore those sidelines in this study. Let's get things underway with 1.e4 d5.}
1. e4 d5 2. exd5 {White takes. We'll recapture.} 2... Qxd5 3. Nc3 {White attacks our queen. We'll move it to a5. There's no changes up to this point. Move 2 and 3 sidelines will be explored in the next chapters. [%csl Rd5]} 3... Qa5 4. Nf3 {4. Nf3 is still the most popular move. 5. d4 is the main continuation thereafter. But sometimes White may play 4. Nf3 without 5. d4, and that's what we need to prepare against. We'll continue with our standard manner of development and bring the knight out to f6.} (4. Bc4 {White may choose to play the bishop out at this point. At this early stage, there are about 1,300,000 games in which 4. Bc4 has been played in a mere 7% of total games. Our approach doesn't change, and we first bring out the kingside knight.} 4... Nf6 5. d3 {White defends their bishop with d3, without committing to d4. The engine suggests a very interesting move ...a6, which will later prove to be really useful.} 5... a6 6. Bd2 {The first move we have to examine is if White chooses to put their bishop opposite our queen. I advise we get our queen out of the potential attack and give a check on e5.} (6. Nf3 {White could also simply develop their knight.} 6... Bg4 {We'll pin the knight on f3. A quick ...Nc6 and 0-0-0 is a great way to develop the entire queenside. If h3, we've got ...Qh5. [%cal Gb8c6,Ga5h5,Ge8c8] [%csl Rf3]}) (6. Ne2 {White could try to get creative with their minor piece moves. A move like this completely ignores our ...b5 idea, though.} 6... b5 7. Bb3 {The bishop is forced back.} 7... Bb7 {We develop, with a tempo on g2. We're going to be developing with ...Nbd7, e6, Be7 and 0-0. White's idea of d3 has proved ineffective now that we've brought the bishop to b7. [%cal Ge7e6,Gb8d7,Gf8e7,Ge8g8] [%csl Rg2]}) 6... Qe5+ 7. Nge2 {The best move is for White to block with the knight. Now the ...a6 move comes into action, and we push forward on the queenside with b5.} (7. Qe2 {If the queen blocks, White is saying they're not interested in playing for a win. Trading is a good idea as Black.} 7... Qxe2+ 8. Ngxe2 {White recaptures.} 8... e6 {We play 8...e6 to cover the f7-pawn. Our queenside pawns will be a valuable asset. With our bishop going to a useful square like b7, we should expect a promising endgame. [%cal Gb7b5,Gc7c5,Gc8b7,Gb8d7]}) 7... b5 8. Bb3 {The bishop goes to b3. ...b5 created a useful square for our bishop. We fianchetto.} 8... Bb7 9. O-O {White castles.} 9... Nbd7 {And we develop our queen's knight. Our queen has gone on quite a journey: from d8 to a5, and from a5 to e5. Despite this, it's an active member of our squad. We will play ...e6 and ...Be7 to castle kingside. White doesn't have an advantage out of the opening. [%cal Ge7e6,Gf8e7,Ge8g8]}) 4... Nf6 5. Bc4 {5. Bc4 is played 22% of the time in around 550,000 games. White's idea is to pressure f7, but they're not committing to d4 yet. We should bring our knight out and prepare to castle queenside. [%csl Rf7]} (5. Be2 {White may simply be interested in developing the bishop. This is played 10% of the time in around 250,000 games. Once again, we can opt for aggression with 5...Nc6 and quick ...e5, Bg4 and 0-0-0 ideas.} 5... Nc6 6. O-O {White castles. We should continue with our plan of ...Bg4, as we want to castle queenside and put pressure along the d-file.} 6... Bg4 7. d3 {This is why White is advised to play the timid 7. d3. The d-pawn would be a target on d4. Let's not castle right away, since White could have some annoying tricks with Ng5, hitting f7.} 7... e6 8. Bd2 {White could place their bishop opposite our queen. We can move it out of the way and apply more pressure on the knight. [%csl Rf3]} 8... Qf5 9. h3 {White may attack our bishop. [%csl Rg4]} 9... h5 {But we're not moving it away! Instead, we have the h5-expansion. It's obviously hope chess to think they'll take on g4, but the move h5 is useful in terms of expanding for a potential kingside attack. White could start a series of attacks on the queen with moves like Nh4. We'd have to move to a safe square like e5. If White doesn't do this, we can go ahead and play normally with ...Bc5 and 0-0-0. [%cal Ge8c8,Gf8c5] [%csl Gg4]}) (5. h3 {White may choose to prevent ...Bg4. This is a good idea seen in 50,000 games, which is 2% of the games in the database from that position. We can still bring the bishop out to f5. [%csl Rg4]} 5... Bf5 6. Bc4 {White puts their bishop on c4. With 6...e6, we block the attack on the f7-square and prepare the development of the dark-squared bishop. [%csl Rf7]} 6... e6 7. O-O {White castles.} 7... c6 {I recommend going for a classical setup. Since White has castled, there is no point in the Nc6-e5 idea we had in the 5. Bc4 and h3 variation. [%cal Gf8e7,Gb8d7,Ge8g8]}) 5... Nc6 6. O-O {White castles, which has been played in 42% of times. We should play 6...Bg4 if given the chance.} (6. h3 {This move seeks to prevent ...Bg4 and so have a stable knight on f3. With ...Ne5, we can force some trades that aren't in White's favor.} 6... Ne5 7. Nxe5 {White will most likely trade. We'll recapture with check.} 7... Qxe5+ 8. Qe2 {The queen blocks. Being behind in development, trades are usually in our favor.} 8... Qxe2+ 9. Bxe2 {The bishop recaptures.} 9... Bf5 {Because there isn't a knight on f3, we can play ...e5 at some point later on. We do want to castle as quickly as possible so that our rook is active on the d-file. White's initial opening advantage has evaporated. [%cal Ge8c8,Ge7e5]}) (6. d4 {White may end up playing d4. There are some dynamic chances after we bring out the bishop to g4.} 6... Bg4 7. h3 {White can try attacking our bishop to force it away.} 7... Qh5 {But we can create a second pin with 7...Qh5! We'll play 0-0-0 and e5 to put pressure on White's center. We have now transposed to Chapter 4, Study 1, where a full analysis of the position can be found. [%cal Ge8c8,Ge7e5] [%csl Rf3]}) 6... Bg4 7. h3 {White could try breaking the pin right away. We'll keep the pin and retreat the bishop. [%csl Rf3,Rg4,Rd1]} 7... Bh5 8. Bb5 {Seeing that e5 is an idea, White may play this strange Bb5 move. Therefore, we won't play ...e5, but nevertheless our dark-squared bishop should get developed. [%csl Rc6]} (8. d4 {d4 at this moment isn't favorable. We will act on it quickly and castle queenside, after which the d-pawn becomes a problem for White. [%csl Rd4]} 8... O-O-O 9. Be3 {Something natural like 9. Be3 fails. Now, we can add another attacker to the d4-pawn by playing e5. [%csl Rd4]} 9... e5 10. d5 {White will most likely push the pawn.} 10... e4 {And we attack White's pinned piece. White has to play g4 to break the pin. Once this happens, we can take on f3 and White will take on h5. White's structure in front of their king will be ruined. [%cal Gg2g4,Re4f3,Rg4h5] [%csl Gf3,Gh5]}) (8. g4 {If this move is played, our queen on a5 can become active. We'll sacrifice on g4.} 8... Nxg4 9. hxg4 {White recaptures.} 9... Bxg4 {This sacrifice works. We have two pawns for the piece, and an attack. We want to play ...e6 to get the bishop involved with Bd6, while our queen can swing over to the kingside and put more pressure on the pinned knight. [%cal Ga5h5,Ga5f5,Ge8c8,Ge7e6,Gf8d6] [%csl Rf3]}) 8... e6 {We are not afraid of White taking on c6 and giving up the bishop pair. If White plays the most natural move, i.e. 9.d4, we'll continue with Bb4, developing and putting pressure on the c3-knight. [%cal Gf8b4] [%csl Rc3]} *

[Chapter "Move 4 Sidelines After 3.Nc3"]
[Study "Other 4th Pawn Moves and 4.Rb1"]
[Video "/downloader/downloads/The Scandinavian Defense/05-02 Move 4 Sidelines After 3.Nc3 - Other 4th Pawn Moves and 4.Rb1.mp4"]
[Orientation "black"]

{On move 4, White could go for unconventional pawn moves. This is what this study is all about: 4. g3, 4. d3 and odd ones like 4. b4 and 4. a3. All of these have their ideas, which we will promptly deal with. We begin with 1.e4 d5.}
1. e4 d5 2. exd5 {White takes on d5. We'll recapture.} 2... Qxd5 3. Nc3 {White attacks the queen. We'll move to a5. [%csl Rd5]} 3... Qa5 4. d3 {4. d3 is the most popular pawn move sideline. 10% of games in the database on the Scandi go like this. I suggest we simply develop with the normal Nf6.} (4. b4 {This gambit has been played 3% of the time. It's an attempt to distract us and activate the rook. We can accept the gambit and challenge White to prove their initiative with Rb1. [%csl Ra5]} 4... Qxb4 5. Rb1 {The rook goes to b1. d6 is a safe square for the time being. [%csl Rb4]} (5. Nb5 {White's idea is to prevent Qd6 and to attack c7. We should move the queen and defend. [%csl Rc7]} 5... Qa5 6. Bc4 {White usually develops the bishop to c4. 83% of games go like this. [%csl Rf7]} (6. Qh5 {A trappy move. White wants to play a discovered attack with Nd6, so we simply defend our queen with ...Nc6. [%csl Ra5]} 6... Nc6 7. Bc4 {7. Bc4 threatens f7. With 7...e6 we block the attack and get ready to continue our development. [%csl Rf7]} 7... e6 8. Bb2 {White has great diagonals for their bishops. However, we're catching up by playing really natural moves that won't be hard for you to remember. Let's push back the troublesome knight on b5. [%csl Gb2,Gc4]} 8... a6 9. Bc3 {This is a one-move attack. We can move out of the way. [%csl Ra5]} 9... Qb6 10. Na3 {This is White's only move. We cannot take on a3 because we would lose g7. [%csl Rg7]} 10... Nf6 {But we can block the bishop's diagonal with tempo. Black is completely winning. [%cal Rf8a3] [%csl Ra3]}) 6... Nf6 {We can go 6...Nf6 and enjoy the position, up a pawn. This move prevents any Qh5 ideas. [%csl Rh5]}) 5... Qd6 6. Nf3 {White develops the other knight. We should develop our king's knight as well.} 6... Nf6 7. d4 {They have to compensate for their pawn down position now. Nb5 and Bf4 ideas could be annoying later, so let's prevent them with a6. [%csl Rb5]} 7... a6 8. Bd3 {The bishop goes to d3 and controls e4.} 8... g6 {I like this fianchetto because our dark-squared bishop will aim at d4 and be relevant in the middlegame. White has a bit more space, but we don't have any weaknesses. In my opinion, the b-file is not enough long-term compensation. [%cal Gf8g7,Ge8g8]}) (4. a3 {The idea behind 4. a3 is to push b4. 4...c6 at this point will allow us to bring the queen back to c7 if b4 is played. [%cal Gb2b4]} 4... c6 5. b4 {This move is to be expected. Our queen drops back. [%csl Ra5]} 5... Qc7 6. Nf3 {White brings their other knight out. We'll continue with the classic Scandi developing moves.} 6... Nf6 7. Bc4 {White develops their bishop and aims at f7. There aren't any tactics on f7, so we can pin with ...Bg4. [%csl Rf7]} 7... Bg4 8. O-O {White castles.} 8... e6 {And now e6 to open up our bishop. We'll want to go for ...Bd6 for pressure on h2. ...Nbd7 is also necessary to get our last piece developed. We want to castle kingside to make sure White's queenside expansion lacks relevance in the middlegame. [%cal Gf8d6,Gb8d7,Ge8g8] [%csl Rh2]}) (4. g3 {This move is quite strange since the light-squared bishop does have a useful square to go to, like c4. I suggest we continue developing and see what White intends beyond Bg2 and piece development.} 4... Nf6 5. Bg2 {White fianchettoes. Now Nc6 is good for quick development.} 5... Nc6 6. Nge2 {Since White prides themselves in having a bishop on g2, they may develop the knight to e2 so that they don't block the bishop. Let's surprise White with the most aggressive continuation, the unexpected h5!} (6. Nf3 {This move isn't played that often. It blocks the bishop on g2. Let's target the fianchetto setup with the aggressive h5. [%csl Rg3,Rg2]} 6... h5 7. O-O {It's possible that White simply ignores our advance on the kingside and castles. So let's keep pushing! ...h4 opens up the kingside.} (7. d3 {White could delay castling and play d3 to develop their bishop. We'll push forward with h4.} 7... h4 8. Nxh4 {White takes. We won't continue with ...Rxh4. Instead, we'll attack the queen.} 8... Bg4 {If we provoke f3, White weakens themselves and all we have to do is retreat to d7. If 9. Bf3, we play ...Bh3, temporarily preventing castling. Our idea is to be annoying with the bishop and induce weaknesses. [%cal Gf2f3,Gg4d7] [%csl Rd1]}) (7. h4 {White could overreact to us wanting to play ...h4 and they could play it themselves to block our potential advance. This is yet another pawn pushed in front of the king's castling spot.} 7... Bg4 {Since h3 to eject our bishop isn't possible, this pin is quite uncomfortable. We'll castle queenside and add pressure to the pinned knight with ...Qf5. We can even consider ...Nb4 tricks to overload the queen and attack c2. [%cal Ge8c8,Ga5f5,Gc6b4] [%csl Rf3,Rc2]}) (7. h3 {7. h3 is another idea. White wants us to play ...h4 so they can play g4 and lock things down.} 7... g5 {But since our queen on a5 defends g5, we can aim for g4. White is under some pressure and the engine is already starting to give Black the advantage. Since g5 opens up the g7 square, we'll develop with ...Bg7 and Be6. [%cal Gg5g4,Gf8g7,Gc8e6]}) 7... h4 8. Nxh4 {White takes the pawn. This is exactly what we want. We're not winning or anything immediate, but our attack is making progress.} 8... Bg4 9. Bf3 {White's best choice is to propose a bishop trade, since f3 creates a weakness. The light-squared bishop is a strong defender, so let's accept the trade.} 9... Bxf3 10. Nxf3 {The knight recaptures, avoiding 10...g5.} 10... O-O-O {But we can castle queenside and still have good attacking prospects in the position. We'll be going Qf5 and Qh3 almost no matter what, and e5 to control the center. [%cal Ga5f5,Gf5h3,Ge7e5]}) 6... h5 7. h3 {The best move for White to defend is h3. If we push h4, they'll play g4.} 7... Bd7 {We certainly don't want them to lock the structure down on the kingside, so we'll continue with development by castling queenside and playing e5. We will aim to take advantage of the g3 and h3 pawn moves. [%cal Ge7e5,Ge8c8,Ga5f5]}) (4. Rb1 {The last move I want us to explore is 4. Rb1. The standard 4...c6 works here. But I like 4...c5 to fight for the center.} 4... c5 5. a3 {White may insist on b4. We should just develop since we cannot stop it anyway. [%cal Gb2b4]} 5... Nf6 6. b4 {White pushes with b4. We'll go backwards to c7, a great square for the queen. [%csl Ra5]} 6... Qc7 7. Nf3 {White develops. Let's get our queen's knight out.} 7... Nc6 8. bxc5 {If they take on c5, we'll have harmonious development after ...e5.} 8... e5 {We're going to take on c5 and castle. If Nb5, we can go Qa5 or even Qd8. We should have virtually no problems in this position. [%cal Ge8g8,Rf8c5] [%csl Gc5]}) 4... Nf6 5. Bd2 {85% of games continue like this. White places the bishop on d2 and eyes the queen. We can develop both knights since the bishop does not pose a threat yet.} 5... Nc6 6. Ne4 {The most popular variation goes like this. White makes use of the one-mover available to them to attack our queen. b6 is a safe square for our queen. [%csl Ra5]} 6... Qb6 7. Nxf6+ {White takes on f6 with check. This was likely to happen from even before Ne4 was played. The knight did not have a solid outpost on e4, nor any prospects.} 7... exf6 {In essence, we have a great Caro-Tartakower variation, where White's pieces are bad. Material is equal, but we're already -1.2. Moves like ...Bc5 and 0-0 are desirable. White will have a tough time handling the pressure on f2 and b2. [%cal Gc8e6,Gf8c5] [%csl Rg1,Rf1,Rd2,Rd1,Rf2,Rb2]} *

[Chapter "Move 3 Sidelines"]
[Study "Lines After 3.Nf3"]
[Video "/downloader/downloads/The Scandinavian Defense/06-01 Move 3 Sidelines - Lines After 3.Nf3.mp4"]
[Orientation "black"]

{In chapter 6, we'll take a look at the move 3 deviations by White. A lot of White players assume Black is only prepared for 3.Nc3, so they want to catch them off guard with 3.Nf3, for example, which is the second most popular move, and we'll cover it in this study. Let's get started with 1. e4 d5.}
1. e4 d5 2. exd5 {White takes on d5. We recapture.} 2... Qxd5 3. Nf3 {And the most popular alternative is 3.Nf3, played 12% of the time, which is still almost 16 million games in the database! We aim to develop the queenside quickly, so starting with ...Bg4 is logical here.} 3... Bg4 4. Be2 {4.Be2 is by far the most common move in this position, played 76% of the time. White continues with their plan, unpins the knight and has already developed the kingside pieces. It doesn't pose us any direct problems, though. We'll continue to develop naturally and play Nc6.} (4. Nc3 {4.Nc3 could be played here, for example, and it is 16% of the time. Our queen is attacked. A queen trade would definitely be beneficial for us. We'll slide the queen to e6 and give a check. [%csl Rd5]} 4... Qe6+ 5. Qe2 {If White blocks with the queen, we want to trade here. We'll be left with no problems in the position whatsoever.} (5. Be2 {If White blocks with the bishop, it's not actually defending the knight on f3 as it's pinned. Which means we can mess up White's structure by capturing on f3.} 5... Bxf3 6. gxf3 {The g-pawn recaptures. Let's continue our development with Nc6.} 6... Nc6 7. d4 {White advances in the center, and we've seen something similar before. We have a great way of pressuring the d4-pawn. We castle queenside.} 7... O-O-O 8. Be3 {The bishop defends it... [%csl Gd4]} 8... Nf6 {And we've got ...Nf6 to continue. Since our queen is on e6, we'll fianchetto the dark-squared bishop next. The pawn on d4 is the main target. [%cal Gg7g6,Gf8g7] [%csl Rd4]}) 5... Qxe2+ 6. Bxe2 {Bishop takes, and now we know what side of the board we want to develop first.} 6... Nc6 7. h3 {They might attack our bishop here. We'll simply move it and keep it relevant. [%csl Rg4]} 7... Bh5 8. g4 {It's logical to chase us away with g4. We'll move to g6. [%csl Rh5]} 8... Bg6 9. Bb5 {A good and active move is Bb5, pinning our knight on c6. We cannot push ...e5 now as it would be hanging, although we need to complete our development. [%csl Rc6]} 9... e6 10. Ne5 {White could capture and ruin our structure, but Ne5 is more active, now threatening to capture on c6 while attacking our bishop on g6. We've got a defense, though. [%csl Rc6,Rg6]} 10... Ne7 {Let's support the c6-knight with a knight on e7 (which is why ...e6 is crucial). If White takes on g6, we'll just recapture with the h-pawn. If not, we'll castle long, play ...f6 to kick the knight and at some point undermine White's extended structure on the kingside with ...h5. [%cal Ge8c8,Gf7f6,Gh7h5] [%csl Gc6,Ge7]}) 4... Nc6 5. O-O {The most popular move is 5.O-O, played 43% of the time here. White aimed for a quick kingside development and we aim for a quick queenside development. We go for opposite-side castling.} (5. h3 {The h3-push on move 5 is played 8% of the time. But we've got a solution to this as well.} 5... Bxf3 6. Bxf3 {Where will we place the queen when White recaptures? In many of these positions, it's too slow if we don't play with tempo like this. [%csl Rd5]} 6... Qe6+ 7. Qe2 {Once White blocks, we don't want to take right away. Their light-squared bishop might be too strong in the future if we do that, and in any case we have a handy way of improving our position by playing Nd4.} 7... Nd4 8. Qxe6 {But once they exchange our idea wasn't to recapture the queen right away. We can take on f3 and split their kingside pawns.} 8... Nxf3+ 9. gxf3 {White takes back.} 9... fxe6 {And we'll take the queen now, with a fighting endgame as both sides have a damaged pawn structure. We'll get the knight to f5 next and fianchetto the dark-squared bishop, while aiming to castle long. [%cal Ge8c8,Gg7g6,Gh6f5,Gg8h6,Gf8g7] [%csl Re6,Re7,Rf3,Rf2]}) (5. Nc3 {What if Nc3 now? It's played 25% of the time. I suggest retreating our queen to d7, which is a safe square as we prepare development. [%csl Rd5]} 5... Qd7 6. h3 {A more confrontational move here is h3, attacking the bishop. In this line, we should not retreat but instead trade and play to control the center more. [%csl Rg4]} (6. O-O {For example, 6.O-O does nothing to prevent our plans. We'll castle queenside.} 6... O-O-O 7. d3 {7.d3 is already somewhat passive, but does open up the bishop. We can respond actively.} 7... e5 8. Be3 {White just developing is solid enough, but they are going to find themselves somewhat passive.} 8... f5 {After ...f5, Black is already better. We've got a strong center, a safe king and our knight is coming to f6. [%cal Gg8f6] [%csl Gf5,Ge5]}) 6... Bxf3 7. Bxf3 {Bishop takes, and we can safely castle now.} 7... O-O-O 8. d3 {White advances with d3, since they can't play d4. Nothing stops us from pushing ...e5! [%csl Rd4]} 8... e5 9. Be3 {Once the bishop gets out...} 9... f5 {There's the f-pawn as well. The position is equal, but I would argue that Black has more space and clear prospects of what to do next. That is to activate the knight to f6, the bishop to b4 and shove the c-knight into the central d4-square. If needed, we can slide the king to b8 for safety. [%cal Gc6d4,Gf8b4,Gg8f6,Gc8b8]}) 5... O-O-O 6. h3 {What should we do if White attacks our bishop now? We can offer it in order to open up the h-file for the rook! [%csl Rg4]} (6. Nc3 {If White attacks the queen, we don't neccesarily have to go for the solid ...Qd7. f5 is an active location for the queen.} 6... Qf5 7. h3 {Can you spot the move that answers White if they attack our bishop with h3? [%csl Rg4]} 7... h5 8. hxg4 {Again, White capturing on g4 is a mistake. We'll take and open up the h-file, with a strong attack.} 8... hxg4 9. Nh2 {But if White retreats to h2 now, there is no ...f5 to protect our g4-pawn, nor ...Qh5. But we can sacrifice the pawn with e6, preparing to bring the bishop into the game. It will be very difficult for White to deal with our h-file attack. [%csl Rg4]} 9... e6 10. Bxg4 {White doesn't shy away from grabbing the pawn. Our queen is attacked, but we don't have to retreat it to a passive square.} 10... Qg6 {But we have a big attack and a -1 advantage. After ...Nf6 and ...Bd6 or Bc5 it won't be easy for White to handle the pressure on their kingside. For more analysis, refer back to 6.h3. [%cal Gf8c5,Gg8f6,Gf8d6]}) 6... h5 7. Nc3 {Before potentially capturing on g4, White might attack our queen with Nc3. Retreating would be passive, so let's keep the pressure on with Qf5.} (7. hxg4 {If they take, that's in fact a big blunder. We'll capture on g4 and open the h-file.} 7... hxg4 8. Nh2 {White's best retreating square is h2, attacking the g4-pawn with multiple pieces. We can't let them take it. [%csl Rg4]} 8... f5 {So we'll defend with ...f5, and now our queen has a different route of joining the kingside attack. Despite having an extra piece White is actually losing because of our invasion on the h-file! [%cal Gd5f7,Gf7h5]}) 7... Qf5 8. d3 {The best move is not to capture the bishop but rather to play d3 and try to develop as many pieces as possible first. We'll do the same and continue offering the bishop.} (8. hxg4 {Again, White capturing on g4 is a mistake. We'll take and open up the h-file, with a strong attack.} 8... hxg4 9. Nh2 {But if White retreats to h2 now, there is no ...f5 to protect our g4-pawn, nor ...Qh5. But we can sacrifice the pawn with e6, preparing to bring the bishop into the game. It will be very difficult for White to deal with our h-file attack. [%csl Rg4]} 9... e6 10. Bxg4 {White doesn't shy away from grabbing the pawn. Our queen is attacked, but we don't have to retreat it to a passive square.} 10... Qg6 {But we have a big attack and a -1 advantage. After ...Nf6 and ...Bd6 or Bc5 it won't be easy for White to handle the pressure on their kingside. For more analysis, refer back to 6.h3. [%cal Gf8c5,Gg8f6,Gf8d6]} 11. Kh1 {White wants to play Bh3 to block the h-file without allowing ...Rxh3. And if we're slow and let them do that, they'll gain the advantage. Our c6-knight comes into the action now, and attacks the c2-pawn.} 11... Nd4 12. d3 {White thinks they're out of the woods by blocking our queen and protecting c2. Spoiler: they aren't! We'll take on c2 anyway. The queen is overloaded. [%csl Rc2]} 12... Nxc2 13. Qxc2 {If they capture, their queen isn't defending the bishop anymore. So we capture on g4.} 13... Qxg4 14. Nb5 {They can threaten checkmate with Nb5. There is a straightforward defense, however. [%csl Rc7]} 14... Bd6 15. Nxd6+ {White takes...} 15... Rxd6 {And once we recapture with the rook, Black is winning here. Our queen is still coming to the h-file, the knight is going to activate on f6, and now we can also play ...Rd5 and ...Rdh5 for a three-piece stack on the h-file! Too much for White to deal with. [%cal Gg4h4,Gg8f6,Gd6d5,Gd5h5]}) 8... Nf6 9. Be3 {White activates the dark-squared bishop instead. Let's gain some central space with e5. Again, the sacrifice still works.} 9... e5 10. Ng5 {White wants to trade with Ng5! This move opens up the two light-squared bishops, and White attacks our f7-pawn at the same time. So, we have to react. [%csl Rf7]} 10... Bxe2 11. Qxe2 {Once the queen recaptures, there is an active way to defend f7.} 11... Nd5 {We can centralize the knight on d5 and offer a trade. Our queen is covering the f7-pawn now, and if White doesn't trade on d5 we'll get our knights to d4 and f4. Eventually we will push ...f6 and possibly ...g5 and ...g4 as well. We have better control of the center and a potentially powerful pawn storm. [%cal Gf7f6,Gg7g5,Gc6d4,Gd5f4]} *

[Chapter "Move 3 Sidelines"]
[Study "White Plays 4.Be2 and 5.d4"]
[Video "/downloader/downloads/The Scandinavian Defense/06-02 Move 3 Sidelines - White Plays 4.Be2 and 5.d4.mp4"]
[Orientation "black"]

{I want us to look at more 3.Nf3 lines. There's a less common variation, and that includes White playing 4.Be2 like in the previous study but also 5.d4, the direct central pawn break. We have to know how to fight such a simple and straightforward assault. Let's get things underway with 1.e4 d5.}
1. e4 d5 2. exd5 {White takes on d5. We'll recapture with the queen.} 2... Qxd5 3. Nf3 {White wants to develop the kingside first. I suggest we pin the knight right away.} 3... Bg4 4. Be2 {White continues with their plan, unpins the knight and has already developed their kingside pieces. It doesn't pose us any direct problems, though. We'll continue to develop our queenside.} 4... Nc6 5. d4 {5.d4 is played only 10% of the time here, but it might be the most instructive move. White simply puts a pawn in the center and wants to develop further. We'll go for our usual idea here and castle queenside. [%csl Gd4]} 5... O-O-O 6. c3 {6.c3 defends the center, but is slow and gives exactly what we want. With ...e5, we strike directly at the white center. [%csl Gd4,Gc3]} (6. c4 {A natural move is to take advantage of our queen still being on d5 and attack it with c4. Instead of the usual a5, I suggest we slide our queen to f5. [%csl Rd5]} 6... Qf5 7. Be3 {7.Be3 is somewhat more natural than 7.O-O, and it protects the d4-pawn in the center. However, we've got a tactic! We'll start by taking on f3. [%csl Rd4]} (7. O-O {7.O-O is possible for White. If you see this move, you might be playing against an experienced or a prepared opponent who is aware of the problems in the 7.Be3 line. White offers the d4-pawn, and we will grab it. [%csl Rd4]} 7... Nxd4 8. Nxd4 {The white knight takes, and there's tension between the pieces. We don't mind a few trades. [%csl Rf5,Re2]} 8... Bxe2 9. Qxe2 {The queen takes, and now we get our piece back on d4. [%csl Rd4]} 9... Rxd4 10. Be3 {And White will do anything to attack our pieces with tempo. Also, the a7-pawn will be under attack once the rook moves back. [%csl Rd4,Ra7]} 10... Rd8 11. Nc3 {Meanwhile, White is getting a lead in development, which was their point when sacrificing a pawn. As we are behind, let's do something about it and open up our kingside pieces. We need to prioritize development over trying to hold onto the a7-pawn. For example, Kb8 leaves us in dire trouble after either white rook comes to d1. [%csl Rf8,Rg8,Rh8]} 11... e6 12. Rad1 {White wants to take control of the d-file. We don't have a better option than to accept the trade.} 12... Rxd1 13. Rxd1 {White is dominating the d-file. Our main goal is still to develop as quickly as we can. ...a6 14.Qd2 is one disaster which might befall the unwary.} 13... Nf6 14. Bxa7 {White can grab the pawn here and shift the material back to equality. Keep in mind that ...b6 to trap the bishop here doesn't work because of c5 and White is winning. [%cal Gc4c5,Rb7b6]} 14... Be7 {We can develop to e7. The material is equal and both sides have activated almost all the pieces now. Our rook is coming to d8, with an equal position but a lot still to happen. [%cal Gh8d8]}) 7... Bxf3 8. Bxf3 {White takes. The d4-pawn seems to be covered, but in reality it isn't. We can capture with the knight! [%csl Rd4]} 8... Nxd4 9. Bxd4 {White accepts the piece sacrifice. Now there is a forcing sequence which regains the piece, starting with a queen check.} (9. Bg4 {Instead of taking the free piece in the center, White could try to go for the pin with Bg4. However, we won't lose our queen, since we have a check with the knight which also discovers an attack on the white queen! [%csl Rf5,Rc8]} 9... Nc2+ 10. Qxc2 {If White takes our knight, they lose the guard of the bishop.} 10... Qxg4 11. O-O {From here on, their main idea will be to push on the queenside and try to create an attack. Let's try to solidify our queenside.} 11... a6 12. b4 {Be ready to face b4. Ideally, we want to place our knight on f5. [%cal Gb4b5]} 12... Nh6 13. b5 {White is persistent with b5, though. And here's a good defensive move to remember.} 13... Qg6 {After ...Qg6, we've got nothing to be afraid of. We offer a queen trade, which would neutralize White's attack immediately, and we cover the a6-square. Our knight is still coming to f5 and Black is slightly better. [%cal Gh6f5] [%csl Gf5]}) 9... Qe6+ 10. Be2 {White blocks with the bishop and covers c4. But the bishop on d4 is pinned and vulnerable. [%csl Gd8,Rd4,Rd1]} 10... Qe4 11. O-O {The best White can do is to castle. Let's grab the bishop. [%csl Rd4]} 11... Qxd4 12. Qa4 {Much better than exchanging queens is to slide to a4. This way, White claims they've got compensation for the pawn in the form of activity. Their bishop is coming to f3 and their knight is coming to c3. We have to find a way to develop as well, and we'll start with ...e6. [%cal Gb1c3,Ge2f3]} 12... e6 13. Nc3 {White's best move is Nc3 here, and the position can become poisonous for us in a split second if we're not precise. For example, White is threatening Nb5 here already, as well as to put a rook on d1. We have to stop the bigger of those threats, which is Nb5. [%cal Gc3b5] [%csl Ra7,Rd4]} 13... a6 14. Rad1 {We can't stop the other idea, however! White establishes control of the d-file and attacks our queen. Definitely we have to deal with a lot for the pawn. Let's retreat to e5, with the clear idea of activating our pieces to f6 and d6 next. [%csl Rd4]} (14. Bf3 {Another common improving move is 14.Bf3. It's a good idea to preemptively get our queen off the d-file and possibly offer a queen trade with ...Qb4. [%csl Rb7]} 14... Qc5 15. Rad1 {White still needs a rook on the d-file. They'd love to take on d8, so we should trade first.} 15... Rxd1 16. Rxd1 {The f-rook recaptures, and now there's the threat of Qe8 checkmate! [%cal Ga4e8] [%csl Re8]} 16... Nf6 {But we've got ...Nf6 here to start developing the kingside and cover the e8-square. Next, we'll get the bishop out to e7 and contest the d-file with the rook. Black is slightly better. [%cal Gf8e7,Gh8d8] [%csl Ge8]}) 14... Qe5 15. Rxd8+ {It makes sense for White to trade. Our rooks aren't connected yet. We have to recapture with the king, removing it from the c8-square, where it defends b7.} 15... Kxd8 16. Bf3 {Now Bf3 is even stronger, as it attacks the b7-pawn. Watch out not to hang mate in one here with ...Kc8! We need to move up a gear. Instead of trying to protect the king on the queenside, we'll develop and give up the b7-pawn! [%csl Rb7,Re8]} (16. Rd1+ {If White gives a check, which definitely seems natural... We can get the bishop out to d6 and block, which we wanted to do anyway. Now even their h2-pawn is under attack.} 16... Bd6 17. g3 {17.g3 defends the position, but puts us back in the driver's seat. White has no clear targets right now.} 17... Nf6 {And we'll go ...Nf6 to finish development. Next, we'll completely consolidate with ...Ke7 and even look to push on the kingside with ...h5, using the g3-pawn as a hook. [%cal Gd8e7,Gh7h5] [%csl Gf6,Ge5,Gd6]}) 16... Nf6 17. Bxb7 {The idea is, after White captures...} 17... Bc5 {We can also get the bishop out and suddenly our kingside is developed! Our king will find shelter in the center and we suddenly have our own attack against White's kingside based on our dark square control. [%csl Gf6,Ge5,Gc5]}) (6. Be3 {6.Be3 defends the center but allows our main idea which we've seen in the previous chapters of the course. [%csl Rd4]} 6... e5 7. c4 {In this variation, White can throw in the move c4 to attack our queen while it's on d5. The queen will go to a familiar square with a tempo. [%csl Rd5]} 7... Qa5+ 8. Bd2 {Once the bishop blocks, I want to show you something revolutionary. We can go for ...Bb4, but instead we have a move which will undermine White's hanging central pawns. Qa6 leaves White with questions regarding the pressure on both central pawns. [%csl Ra5,Rd4,Rc4]} 8... Qa6 9. c5 {The most logical move for White is probably c5, attacking the queen. We don't have to move the queen right now, since we can capture on f3, and if White goes for Bxa6, we can capture their queen. [%csl Ra6]} (9. d5 {If d5 is pushed, with the idea of kicking our knight away, we should take on f3 first. [%csl Rc6]} 9... Bxf3 10. Bxf3 {White recaptures on f3. ...Nd4 is extremly powerful now. [%csl Rd4]} 10... Nd4 11. b3 {Let's demonstrate the prospects of our knight if White defends with b3. It would be great if we could threaten a fork on c2. We swing the queen over to the kingside. [%csl Rc2]} 11... Qg6 12. O-O {If White castles, ...Nc2 isn't as potent anymore because it isn't check, and White has Bh5. We can prevent the troublesome bishop move by bringing our knight to f6.} 12... Nf6 13. Nc3 {White develops and defends. As a response, we've got a move that gains space in the center.} 13... e4 14. Be2 {The bishop was under attack and will retreat to e2... [%cal Gf8d6]} 14... Bd6 {After which we can bring the final minor piece into the game. The main goal has become to attack on the kingside and play against the weak h2-pawn! We'll do that with moves like ...h5, ...Ng4 and we can slide the h-rook to the center for activity. An equal position, but I'd rather have Black here! [%cal Gh7h5,Gf6g4,Gh8e8] [%csl Rh2]}) 9... Bxf3 10. gxf3 {They'll capture on f3 instead and preserve the attack on our queen. Our queen seems to be trapped, but we have a strong defensive move with b5!} 10... b5 11. cxb6 {White capturing en passant doesn't pose us any problems whatsoever. We play the obvious recapture.} (11. dxe5 {We love to see 11.dxe5. White simply splits their pawns. We capture and bring a piece out at the same time. [%csl Re5,Rc5]} 11... Bxc5 12. Nc3 {A natural-looking move here is Nc3, attacking the b5-pawn. We don't need to defend it. There is an aggressive method of counterplay open to us, starting with Nxe5.} 12... Nxe5 13. Bxb5 {If White takes on b5, that's a losing blunder.} 13... Qe6 {We'll slide to e6 and White is busted. They will probably have to play Ne4 to evade discoveries for the moment, in which case we've got ...Nf6. If White castles here, we've got ...Rxd2 followed by ...Nxf3+ to win the queen! [%csl Ge5,Re1]}) (11. d5 {Pushing the pawn might look like a blunder, but it's not! We mustn't capture on d5 because of Nc3. [%cal Gb1c3] [%csl Rd5]} 11... Nd4 {But we can jump to the center instead. We'll aim to capture on c5 on the next move, as well as activate our knight to e7. All in all, an unclear position. [%cal Rf8c5] [%csl Gc5]}) 11... Qxb6 12. dxe5 {The d4-pawn was under serious pressure, but now our rook is open and pinning the bishop on d2. Bb4 adds more pressure. [%csl Rd2]} 12... Bb4 13. Nc3 {Once White blocks...} 13... Nge7 {We can develop the g-knight to e7. We'll take on e5 next. Again, an unclear position, but White's center is gone. [%cal Gc6e5] [%csl Re5]}) (6. O-O {6.O-O might be blitzed out by some players. You can look at it either as a blunder or as a sacrifice of the d4-pawn! We take on f3. [%csl Rd4,Rf3]} 6... Bxf3 7. Bxf3 {White recaptures. And now we've got ...Qxd4 to win the central pawn. [%csl Rd4]} 7... Qxd4 8. Qe2 {If they avoid the queen trade and slide to e2 instead...} 8... Qe5 {Black is better after ...Qe5, offering a trade again. We want to get rid of that bishop on f3 and have ...Nd4 in mind if White doesn't take on e5. [%cal Gc6d4] [%csl Re2]}) (6. Nc3 {White could try and attack our queen with Nc3. But the knight blocks the c-pawn, and thus making it harder for White to defend the d4-pawn! We can get into a familiar position with ...Qa5. [%csl Rd5]} 6... Qa5 7. Be3 {7.Be3 defends the center. Natural development follows. [%csl Rd4]} 7... Nf6 8. O-O {For example, if White doesn't think and castles here...} 8... e5 {Black is much better after ...e5. The d4-pawn is under a lot of pressure and White will most likely lose it. [%csl Rd4]}) 6... e5 7. Be3 {The bishop is required to come and help out with the defense of the center... [%csl Rd4]} 7... Nge7 {But White will need to be careful after we develop the knight to e7. If our knight reaches the f5-square on the next move, White might get into trouble! [%cal Ge7f5] [%csl Re3,Rd4]} *

[Chapter "Move 3 Sidelines"]
[Study "Other Move 3 Sidelines"]
[Video "/downloader/downloads/The Scandinavian Defense/06-03 Move 3 Sidelines - Other Move 3 Sidelines.mp4"]
[Orientation "black"]

{In the second study of Chapter 6, we'll see what White can play apart from Nf3 on move 3. You won't come across these moves as often as Nf3, but it's important to know how to deal with them, as our approach will change depending on what White plays sometimes. It's never good to just blitz out the same moves you learned independent of White's responses! Let's begin with the customary 1.e4 d5.}
1. e4 d5 2. exd5 {White takes the pawn. We'll recapture.} 2... Qxd5 3. d4 {3.d4 is played 8% of the time in this position. White doesn't activate either knight but rather places a pawn in the center. This allows for an interesting idea I want you to remember. We're going to directly strike at the white center with our e-pawn! [%csl Gd4]} (3. c4 {3.c4 is played 3% of the time, and the idea is to grab some central space while attacking our queen. Some players prefer this to Nc3. But it has its downsides, of course. [%csl Rd5]} 3... Qe4+ {For example, we've got the e4-square now to give a check on, and White has to react already. This wouldn't have been possible after 3.Nc3. Now: 1. If 4.Be2, we take g2. 2. If 4.Ne2, we take c4. 3. If 4.Qe2, trade, and play a normal game, castling either way. [%csl Re1]}) (3. Qf3 {3.Qf3 is played only 1% of the time, but that is still almost 2 million games in the database! White would like to trade our queen off from the center and recapture with the knight. We'll keep the queens on.} 3... Qd6 4. Nc3 {White activates the queenside knight. We'll respond by developing as well.} 4... Nf6 5. Bc4 {Once the bishop gets out...} 5... Nc6 {It's important to develop the other knight to c6. With White's queen being in the center, this knight can often jump to d4 or e5 to create trouble. Also, White can't really play d4, so we'll go ...e5 at some point. All in all, 3.Qf3 is nothing to worry about. [%cal Gc6e5,Gc6d4,Ge7e5]}) 3... e5 4. dxe5 {White will capture the pawn here 23% of the time, thinking we'll just recapture and they'll get some tempi. But this is an inaccuracy. We can exchange queens and misplace the white king. [%csl Rd1]} (4. Nf3 {4.Nf3 is the most popular move at this point, defending the center and attacking e5. I suggest we relieve the tension now and take on d4. [%csl Gd4,Re5]} 4... exd4 5. Qxd4 {If White recaptures with the queen, it means they would really like to trade and play an endgame they can't be worse in. Let's try to keep the queens on.} (5. Nxd4 {If White recaptures with the knight, there's an interesting developing idea. We don't want to activate the knight to c6 immediately and offer a trade. Instead, we'll give a check on b4.} 5... Bb4+ 6. c3 {It's logical to block with the pawn and attack the bishop. [%csl Rb4]} (6. Nc3 {If White blocks with the knight, we've got nothing to worry about.} 6... Ne7 {We'll just get our knight to e7 to support ...Nbc6 next. Our queen and bishop are doing a great job on the diagonals, making it hard for White to develop. [%cal Gb8c6,Ge8g8] [%csl Gd5,Gb4,Rc3,Rg2]}) 6... Bc5 {Let's retreat to c5. We attack the knight and would like to capture on d4 with the bishop if allowed. Next, we'll develop the knight to e7 and castle short. [%cal Gg8e7,Ge8g8] [%csl Rd4]}) 5... Qe6+ 6. Be3 {If White blocks with the bishop, that makes it easy for us to continue.} (6. Qe3 {If White blocks with the queen, we can't avoid the trade anymore. But we've got a small trick to accelerate our development.} 6... Bc5 7. Qxe6+ {White will most likely capture here.} 7... Bxe6 {We'll take back with the bishop. It might be boring for you to play a queenless middlegame, but if that's what White wants, this is the most we can draw out of it. A flexible position where we can castle either way. If anyone is better here, it's Black. [%cal Ge8c8,Ge8g8,Gg8f6]}) 6... Nc6 {We'll be the ones developing the knight and attacking the queen now. Our pieces will come to life on their natural squares soon after, and we'll castle long. A comfortable position for Black. [%cal Gf8d6,Gc8d7,Gg8f6,Ge8c8] [%csl Rd4]}) (4. Nc3 {White could go for the peculiar-looking 4.Nc3, attacking our queen at this "inconvenient" moment. On the first glance, White simply gives up the d4-pawn, but it's not so easy. White will trade queens and then use the knight to get some compensation. This move is played 21% of the time. [%csl Gd4]} 4... Qxd4 5. Qxd4 {White trades, and we recapture.} 5... exd4 6. Nb5 {They play Nb5, forking the pawn on d4 and the pawn on c7 and threatening a fork! If we just play a move like ...Kd8, there is Bf4 and we are in trouble. That's why trading the dark-squared bishops is key here. We insert a check on b4 before continuing. [%csl Rc7,Rd4]} 6... Bb4+ 7. Bd2 {Blocking is more confrontational, but we are happy to exchange bishops.} 7... Bxd2+ 8. Kxd2 {Once White recaptures...} 8... Kd8 {Now it's safe to go for ...Kd8 and defend against the fork. Next, we'll play ...c6 to kick the knight away if it doesn't take our central pawn, and activate our own knight to f6. From there, it can check the king on e4 and then reroute to the better d6-square. All in all, our king will be safe on the dark squares like c7 or e7 since the dark-squared bishops have been traded. [%cal Gg8f6,Gf6e4,Ge4d6,Gc7c6]}) 4... Qxd1+ 5. Kxd1 {White has to recapture. And our idea is to develop quickly with a serious initiative, not being in a rush to regain our pawn. [%csl Rd1]} 5... Nc6 6. Bb5 {An active defense is White pinning our knight on c6 and threatening to disrupt our pawn structure. Now, defending with something like ...Bd7 is too passive, I'd say. Let's win some tempi instead, developing with check. [%csl Rc6]} (6. Nf3 {White might blitz out Nf3 here, developing and defending the center. But that already plays into our hands. We'll develop and pin the defender of the e5-pawn. This threatens the pawn, threatens to capture on f3 and allows us to castle long on the next move! [%csl Rf3,Re5]} 6... Bg4 7. Bf4 {White deals with the biggest problem, which is the loose pawn on e5. We'll castle long now, with check!} 7... O-O-O+ 8. Nd2 {The knight blocks, but we keep calling the shots. Let's develop the bishop with an attack on f2 now! The position plays itself - we develop and create threats, move after move. [%csl Rf2]} 8... Bc5 9. Bg3 {It's probably best for White to retreat to g3...} 9... Nge7 {And we'll develop the knight next, with the idea of ...Nf5, attacking that bishop that holds everything together for White. It's extremely easy to play this position. [%cal Ge7f5] [%csl Rg3]}) (6. f4 {6.f4 might look scary, as White gets some space and protects the center with the pawn. But them not having any pieces developed yet might backfire. We can challenge that structure right away with ...f6. [%csl Re5]} 6... f6 7. exf6 {White takes.} 7... Nxf6 {And we'll recapture with the knight, having activated two pieces against White's none. The white king is stranded in the center of the board. Next, our bishops are coming to c5 and either f5 or g4, while our f6-knight can easily jump forward and threaten a fork on f2. Castling long and getting the rooks to the center is natural. Black is better. [%cal Gc8f5,Gf8c5,Gf6e4,Gf6g4,Ge8c8,Gh8e8]}) 6... Bg4+ 7. f3 {White can block with f3 and attack the bishop, but this doesn't mean we'll retreat. We can continue in the same vein and castle queenside, giving check.} 7... O-O-O+ 8. Bd2 {White has to react again. Now we'll bring our bishop back and guard the c6-knight.} 8... Bd7 9. Bxc6 {White will probably have to capture on c6 to get rid of the pressure on the e5-pawn.} 9... Bxc6 {We have the bishop pair and a lead in development, while the white king is still stuck in the center. We have a clear advantage, despite the pawn deficit. [%csl Gf8,Gc6,Gd8,Rd1]} *

[Chapter "Move 2 Sidelines"]
[Study "Move 2 sidelines"]
[Video "/downloader/downloads/The Scandinavian Defense/07-01 Move 2 Sidelines - Move 2 sidelines.mp4"]
[Orientation "black"]

{Welcome to the seventh chapter! We'll take a look at a few of the second moves that White might opt for instead of immediately capturing after the normal opening moves of 1.e4 d5.}
1. e4 d5 2. d3 {White instead supports the e4-pawn with d3, which isn't a very popular move (2% of games), but it isn't illogical either. We can enter a safe endgame with 2...dxe4, but I want to recommend a more fighting approach as well. [%csl Ge4,Gd3]} (2. d4 {White tries to grab central space and gambit a pawn with 2. d4, which is a bit more popular than 2. d3, having a 3% occurrence. [%csl Re4]} 2... dxe4 3. Nc3 {White develops. We can develop and fight for the e4-pawn for now. [%csl Re4]} 3... Nf6 4. f3 {White offers another pawn, so they can speed up their development. We won't help White. We'll develop and support the e-pawn. [%csl Rf3]} 4... Bf5 5. fxe4 {White captures on e4. We take back with the knight.} (5. g4 {White could attack our bishop instead of capturing on e4. We'll retreat to g6, maintaining the connection to the e4-pawn. [%csl Rf5]} 5... Bg6 6. g5 {White just keeps on expanding on the queenside. Now our knight is driven away from the defense of e4. We'll bring it into the center of the board. [%csl Rf6]} 6... Nd5 7. Nxe4 {White captures on e4.} 7... Nc6 {And we answer with development. We've given up our pawn back, but White has weakened their position with pawn pushes. Next up we can explode the center with the aggressive ...e5 or continue with the prosaic ...e6. [%cal Ge7e6,Ge6e5] [%csl Gd5,Rf3,Rg5,Re1]}) 5... Nxe4 6. Qf3 {White brings their queen to f3. Both minor pieces are now attacked. We have to trade on c3. [%csl Rf5,Re4]} 6... Nxc3 7. bxc3 {White recaptures, keeping the bishop and the b7-pawn under pressure. With ...e6, Black supports the bishop on f5 and opens up the other bishop. [%csl Rf5,Rb7]} (7. Qxf5 {If White grabs the bishop...} 7... e6 {...e6 with ...Nd5 next gives Black a comfortable position. White has some practical compensation in the form of open files but we have an extra pawn and aren't even behind in development. [%cal Gc3d5] [%csl Rf5]}) 7... e6 8. Qxb7 {White grabs the queenside pawn. ...Nd7 is mandatory now.} 8... Nd7 9. Nf3 {White develops. Let's ask the queen on b7 a question.} (9. Bb5 {White pins our d7-knight, intending to capture it and force us to take back with the king. Let's kick away the queen. [%csl Rd7]} 9... Rb8 10. Bxd7+ {Looks like we have to move our king out, since the rook would be left hanging if we recaptured with the queen.} 10... Kxd7 11. Qf3 {The queen retreats. We can force a queen trade, which is definitely beneficial for us.} 11... Qh4+ 12. g3 {White blocks.} 12... Qe4+ {The queen has some scope on e4! [%cal Re4h1,Re4c2]}) 9... Rb8 10. Qxa7 {White grabs another pawn.} 10... Bxc2 {We do as well. Black is once again doing very well. We've got the open b-file and easy development for our kingside. [%cal Gf8e7,Ge8h8]}) (2. Nf3 {White goes for this pawn sacrifice, intending to cause some trouble with the knight. Doesn't look very threatening, but it happens in 8% of the games! [%csl Re4]} 2... dxe4 3. Ng5 {White wants to grab the pawn back, so let's protect it! [%csl Re4]} 3... Bf5 4. Nc3 {White attacks it yet again. Let's keep defending and developing. [%csl Re4]} (4. Bc4 {White goes after the f7-pawn. Blocking the diagonal with e6 is natural and good. The g5-knight is now en prise. [%csl Rf7]} 4... e6 5. d3 {White protects it. [%cal Gc1g5]} 5... exd3 {We can take on d3 and simply continue developing. White's pieces aren't doing anything to our position, and we can get our pieces out quickly, with ...Nc6 coming. [%cal Gb8c6,Gd8d1]}) (4. f3 {White pushes f3 to attack e4. We can safely grab on f3 now. [%csl Re4]} 4... exf3 5. Qxf3 {The queen recaptures, hitting f5 and b7. We'll once again carry out this idea from previous moves — sacrificing the b7-pawn for development. [%csl Rf5,Rb7]} 5... e6 6. Qxb7 {White captures on b7.} 6... Nd7 {With ...Rd8 Black is once again doing very well here with a lot of developing options. [%cal Gg8f6,Gf8e7,Ge7d6,Gd6c5,Ga8b8]}) 4... Nf6 5. Bc4 {White attacks the f7-pawn. We are content to block the attack and play e6, preparing further development. [%csl Rf7]} (5. d3 {White offers the d-pawn. Giving it up on e4 wouldn't be the best idea here, so we'll just trade. [%csl Rd3]} 5... exd3 6. Qf3 {White once again uses the Qf3 idea. With ...Qc8, we protect both the b7-pawn and the bishop. [%csl Rf5,Rb7]} 6... Qc8 7. Bxd3 {White recaptures on d3. We may as well trade bishops.} 7... Bxd3 8. Qxd3 {White recaptures.} 8... h6 {We'll attack the knight. Black is doing great here. White is down a pawn for nothing. [%cal Gb8c6,Rh6g5]}) 5... e6 6. Qe2 {White attacks e4. We can keep protecting the d4-pawn by centralizing our queen. [%csl Re4]} 6... Qd4 7. Nb5 {White attacks the queen. [%csl Rd4]} 7... Qb6 {The queen just retreats. We keep the diagonal and White's knights aren't doing anything special. We can kick them back with our pawns if we feel the need. [%cal Gb6g1,Gb8c6,Rc6d4] [%csl Rb5]}) 2... e5 3. exd5 {White trades. We'll recapture and get the queen out.} (3. Nf3 {White attacks the e5-pawn insead of trading. Let's defend it. [%csl Re5]} 3... Nc6 4. Nbd2 {White is simply developing. We'll later want to push the pawn to f6 to solidify the center, so ...Ne7 is logical.} 4... Nge7 5. g3 {White prepares to fianchetto.} 5... f6 6. Bg2 {White fianchettoes.} 6... Bg4 {And we prepare to castle queenside. Our center looks very good! [%cal Gd8d7,Ge8a8] [%csl Ge5,Gd5]}) 3... Qxd5 4. Nc3 {The knight can attack our queen, but we can pin it by developing the bishop. [%csl Rd5]} 4... Bb4 5. Bd2 {White breaks the pin. Let's get rid of the knight to keep our queen in the center.} 5... Bxc3 6. Bxc3 {White recaptures.} 6... Nc6 {And with ...Nc6 we start to develop our minor pieces. We can decide if we castle kingside or queenside. [%cal Gg8e7,Gc8e6,Ge8h8,Ge8a8]} *

