{Finally, I want to conclude with two moves that aren't gambits. 2.Nc3 and 2.e5 give rise to normal positions, but against both of them we can get good development.}
1. e4 d5 2. Nc3 {White immediately brings out the queenside knight and protects e4. Since 2. exd5 is the most popular, we only see 2. Nc3 6% of the time. We'll lure out the knight by trading on e4, though pushing the d-pawn forward is a legitimate alternative. [%csl Re4]} (2. e5 {White chooses to expand in the center with e5 and intends to go d4. We can see this in 8% of games. Our goal is to make d4 harder for White to play.} 2... c5 3. d4 {This move isn't the best, but it's the most popular here, being played nearly 27% of the time. We'll capture because we can gain a tempo on the queen. [%csl Rd4]} (3. Nf3 {With the second most popular move (played nearly 22% in the database), White adds control to the d4-square by developing the knight to f3, also supporting the e5-pawn. By pinning the knight we fight for the d4-square.} 3... Bg4 4. h3 {White attacks our bishop. We can remove the knight which fights for the center. [%csl Rg4]} (4. Be2 {If White breaks the pin...} 4... Nc6 {...both the e5-pawn and the d4-square become weak after ...Nc6. [%csl Re5,Rd4]}) 4... Bxf3 5. Qxf3 {White recaptures.} 5... a6 {We prevent Bb5, and prepare to play ...Nc6 and ...e6. The d4-square is ours! [%cal Gb8c6,Ge7e6] [%csl Gd4]}) (3. f4 {3.f4 aims to defend the e5-pawn. We'll simply develop our knight, since playing ...c5 has given us the c6-square to work with. [%csl Gf4,Ge5]} 3... Nc6 4. Nf3 {White brings their knight out. The f5-square is waiting for our bishop.} 4... Bf5 5. Bb5 {White pins our knight. But even though this pin is a popular idea, many times it'll simply give us the bishop pair after we attack the bishop. [%csl Rc6]} 5... Qb6 6. Bxc6+ {White takes. We'll recapture with the queen.} 6... Qxc6 7. O-O {White castles. Let's complete the central pawn structure.} 7... e6 8. d3 {The pawn goes to d3, controlling e4 and c4 and blunting our bishop. It's White's best attempt to develop normally. We'll develop to e7, intending ...Ng6.} 8... Ne7 9. Nc3 {White brings their knight out.} 9... Ng6 {We have the bishop pair and we'll castle kingside. Do keep an eye out for h3 ideas to trap your bishop: in that case, ...h5 is a good move to prevent this plan. Otherwise, you should feel content about the opening. [%cal Gf8e7,Ge8g8,Gh7h5,Rh2h3]}) (3. c3 {This move has happened the most in the games of masters, but amateurs play it less often (around 17% of times). We'll get the knight out. It adds control over the d4-square. [%csl Rd4]} 3... Nc6 4. d4 {White expands in the center. Before ...e6, the bishop should come out.} 4... Bf5 5. Nf3 {White develops. And now we can go ahead and play ...e6.} 5... e6 6. Be2 {White prepares to castle. By trading on d4, we make a route for the dark-squared bishop.} 6... cxd4 7. cxd4 {White recaptures.} 7... Bb4+ {With ...Bb4+ we develop the bishop. We plan to bring the knight to e7, and the rook to c8 to occupy the c-file. The d4-pawn could also potentially become a target. [%cal Gd8b6,Gg8e7,Ga8c8] [%csl Re1]}) 3... cxd4 4. Qxd4 {White recaptures. We can now develop with a tempo.} 4... Nc6 5. Bb5 {White will usually pin us. In this case, I suggest we merely continue with development, starting with the light-squared bishop as we want to push ...e6 later. [%csl Rc6,Re8]} 5... Bf5 6. Bxc6+ {The most popular continuation involves White taking on c6.} (6. Nc3 {White could simply develop and attack d5. We'll support d5 with the e-pawn. [%csl Rd5]} 6... e6 7. Nf3 {White develops. Even though c2 hangs, we want to develop, too.} 7... Ne7 {That's why we'll support our knight. Black's advantage is starting to become palpable. ...a6 will come with a tempo on the queen. [%cal Ra7a6] [%csl Gc6,Rb5]}) 6... bxc6 7. Nf3 {White develops their knight. And now it's time to complete the strong pawn structure.} 7... e6 8. O-O {Even though they can castle, we can make use of our center pawns.} 8... c5 9. Qa4+ {And since there isn't a light-squared bishop, we will block with the queen. [%csl Re8]} 9... Qd7 10. Qxd7+ {White exchanges queens on d7, and we recapture.} 10... Kxd7 11. Nc3 {Many people may just forget about c2. This is a common blunder. [%csl Rc2]} 11... Bxc2 {Since it's safe and there are no queens on the board, we can take. Black should win this.}) 2... dxe4 (2... d4 3. Nce2 {The knight retreats. Let's expand in the center now.} 3... e5 4. Ng3 {White is trying to develop. By bringing the bishop to e6 we'll also prevent Bc4.} 4... Be6 5. Nf3 {The knight jumps to f3. We've got a nice way of creating a strong pawn chain.} 5... f6 6. Bb5+ {White develops the bishop. We'll block and attack it with c6.} (6. d3 {White might try to open up the dark-squared bishop, but the f1-bishop suffers. Let's further support our central structure.} 6... c5 7. Be2 {White prepares to castle. Our next move ...g6 will nicely restrict White's knights.} 7... g6 8. O-O {The king hides.} 8... Nc6 {We continue development. Our center is perfect and we have more space for our pieces. [%cal Gd8d7] [%csl Ge5,Gd4,Gc5,Gf6]}) 6... c6 7. Ba4 {The bishop retreats. [%cal Ga4b3]} 7... h5 {The center is closed, so we can expand on the kingside now! [%cal Rh5h4] [%csl Rg3]}) 3. Nxe4 {White recaptures. Let's start developing.} 3... Nf6 4. Nxf6+ {White trades. We'll recapture with the e-pawn.} (4. Ng3 {White retreats instead of trading. A common response whenever a knight appears on g3 (or g6) is to push the h-pawn.} 4... h5 5. h4 {White stops our advance. Let's develop a piece with tempo.} 5... Bg4 6. Be2 {White offers a trade.} 6... c5 {With ...c5 we fight for the d4-square. With ...Nc6 and ...Qc7 we can plan to castle queenside! [%cal Gc5d4,Gb8c6,Gd8c7,Ge8b8,Gg7g6,Gf8g7,Rd2d4]}) 4... exf6 5. d4 {White grabs space in the center. We can now bring the bishop out to the h2-b8 diagonal and prepare to castle.} 5... Bd6 6. Bd3 {White develops as well. We castle.} (6. Nf3 {White goes for standard development. We'll castle.} 6... O-O 7. Be2 {White wants to castle as well. Now it's time to occupy the e-file.} 7... Re8 8. O-O {The king gets to safety. The knight can be rerouted via d7 and f8.} 8... Nd7 9. c4 {White expands. [%csl Gd4,Gc4]} 9... b6 {We prepare to fianchetto and fight for the c5-square. We'll continue with ...Nf8 to g6 to improve the knight. [%cal Gc8b7,Gd7f8,Gf8g6] [%csl Rc5]}) 6... O-O 7. Ne2 {White wants to castle. Let's occupy the e-file.} 7... Re8 8. O-O {White gets their king out of the center. We'll develop the bishop and put pressure on the knight.} 8... Bg4 9. f3 {White attacks the bishop. We'll retreat. [%csl Rg4]} 9... Bh5 10. c3 {White supports the d4-pawn. We can now try to chip away at White's center. [%csl Gd4,Gc3]} 10... c5 11. dxc5 {White trades.} 11... Bxc5+ 12. Kh1 {The king steps away.} 12... Nc6 {We'll develop the final piece. Black is doing very well here. We plan to activate the queen and connect the rooks. Our pieces are much more active than White's. [%cal Gd8b6,Ga8d8] [%csl Re2,Rd3]} *
